/* Nelson Textbook data chunk 07/07; generated only from absi/Nelson.zip. */
window.__SHB_NELSON_QUESTIONS__=(window.__SHB_NELSON_QUESTIONS__||[]).concat([{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-036","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"Of the following, the cardiac lesion resulting in increased volume load is","o":["valvular pulmonic stenosis",". patent ductus arteriosus","valvular aortic stenosis","coarctation of the aorta mitral stenosis"],"k":1,"ex":"Left-to-right shunts (ASD, VSD, AV septal defect, PDA) cause volume overload; obstructive lesions (pulmonic stenosis, aortic stenosis, coarctation) cause pressure overload. PDA is the volume-loading lesion among the options.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Valvular pulmonic stenosis – pressure load on RV.\n\n- **B.** Correct (source answer) – PDA, left-to-right shunt/volume load.\n- **C.** Valvular aortic stenosis – pressure load on LV.\n- **D.** Coarctation – pressure load on LV.\n- **E.** Mitral stenosis – obstruction; left atrial pressure load.\n\nSource explanation:\nThe most common lesions in this group are those that cause left-to-right shunting: atrial septal defect, ventricular septal defect (VSD), AV septal defects (AV canal), and patent ductus arteriosus.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR: letters lost for options B and E; positions A–E assumed.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":36,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-037","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Differential Diagnosis","q":"Of the following, the cyanotic cardiac lesion with decreased pulmonary blood flow is","o":["transposition of the great vessels",".single ventricle","truncus arteriosus","tricuspid atresia","total anomalous pulmonary venous return without obstruction"],"k":3,"ex":"Cyanotic lesions with decreased pulmonary blood flow include TOF, pulmonary atresia, tricuspid atresia and obstructed TAPVR. TGA, truncus arteriosus and unobstructed TAPVR have increased pulmonary flow.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** TGA – increased flow.\n\n- **B.** Single ventricle – flow depends on associated pulmonary stenosis; not the best answer (letter lost).\n- **C.** Truncus arteriosus – increased flow.\n- **D.** Correct (source answer) – tricuspid atresia, oligaemic lungs.\n- **E.** TAPVR without obstruction – increased flow.\n\nSource explanation:\nThis group of congenital heart lesions include (tetralogy of Fallot, pulmonary atresia with an intact septum, tricuspid atresia, total anomalous pulmonary venous return with obstruction).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Single ventricle can be oligaemic when pulmonary stenosis is present; the keyed answer is the clearest option.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":37,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-047","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Patients with a small PDA have the following criteria EXCEPT","o":["continuous murmur heard best at the left upper sternal border","normal peripheral pulses","normal pulmonary artery pressure by echocardiography","asmptomatic and live a normal life expectancy","risk for endocarditis is extremely low"],"k":4,"ex":"A small PDA is asymptomatic, with a continuous murmur at the left upper sternal border radiating to the back, normal peripheral pulses, normal heart size and normal pulmonary artery pressure, and normal life expectancy. Endocarditis risk is NOT negligible in small audible PDAs (the very low risk applies to 'silent' PDAs), so the statement of extremely low risk is the exception.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** True – continuous murmur at LUSB.\n\n- **B.** True – normal pulses (no wide pulse pressure).\n- **C.** True – normal PA pressure.\n- **D.** True – asymptomatic, normal life expectancy.\n- **E.** Correct answer (EXCEPT) – endocarditis risk is higher in small audible PDAs than in silent ones (source answer E).\n\nSource explanation:\nA silent PDA is a tiny defect that cannot be heard by auscultation and is only detected by other nonclinical means such as echocardiography. Life expectancy is always normal in this population and the risk for endocarditis is extremely low. Patients with a small PDA have an audible long-ejection or continuous murmur heard best at the left upper sternal border that radiates to the back. In addition, they have normal peripheral pulses. Because there is negligible left to right shunting these patients have normal left aorta (LA) and left ventricle (LV) size and normal pulmonary artery pressure by echocardiography and chest x-ray. These patients like those with silent PDAs are aysmptomatic and live a normal life expectancy. They have a higher risk for endocarditis.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Source explanation is internally contradictory (it says the risk is 'extremely low' for silent PDA and 'higher' for small PDA) but supports the keyed answer. OCR: 'asmptomatic', 'aysmptomatic'.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":47,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-050","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Investigation","q":"All the following are true regarding the diagnosis of coarctation of the aorte ExcEPT","o":["cardiac enlargement are noted with severe coarctation","notching of the ribs is common by late childhood","doppler is useful for demonstrating the specific site of the obstruction","the echocardiogram is equivocal","diagnostic catheterization is mandatory before surgery"],"k":4,"ex":"Echo-Doppler shows the site and gradient of coarctation; chest X-ray shows cardiomegaly in severe cases and rib notching by late childhood; CT/MRI angiography is used when echo is equivocal. Diagnostic catheterisation is NOT mandatory before surgery when anatomy is well defined by echo, CT or MRI.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Cardiac enlargement with severe coarctation – true.\n\n- **B.** Rib notching by late childhood – true.\n- **C.** Doppler shows the site of obstruction – true.\n- **D.** Option text truncated in OCR (begins 'the echocardiogram is equivocal') and its letter is missing – cannot be fully analysed; it presumably refers to CT/MRI use when echo is equivocal, which is not confirmed.\n- **E.** Correct answer (EXCEPT) – catheterisation is not mandatory (source answer E).\n\nSource explanation:\nIn cases that are well defined by echocardiography, CT, or MRl, diagnostic catheterization is not usually required before surgery.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR problem: option D is truncated (first part missing) and has no letter. Not decisive for the answer. Classified accepted but flagged for PDF verification.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":50,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-052","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"Paroxysmal hypercyanotic attacks (hypoxic, \"blue,\" or \"tet\" spells) are a particular problem during the 1st 2 yr of life. They are characterized by","o":["early evening occurrence","an increase in intensity of the systolic murmur","unpredictable onset","metabolic alkalosis","more freguent spells in patients with marked cyanosis at rest"],"k":2,"ex":"Tet spells occur in the first 2 years, typically in the morning on waking or after crying/feeding, with unpredictable onset. The systolic murmur DECREASES or disappears because right-ventricular outflow flow falls; hypoxia produces metabolic ACIDOSIS.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Early evening – wrong; spells are commonest in the morning.\n\n- **B.** Increase in murmur intensity – wrong; murmur decreases.\n- **C.** Correct (source answer) – unpredictable onset.\n- **D.** Metabolic alkalosis – wrong; metabolic acidosis.\n- **E.** More frequent with marked resting cyanosis – wrong; mildly cyanotic infants are more prone.\n\nSource explanation:\nThe spells occur most frequently in the morning on initially awakening or after episodes of vigorous crying. Temporary disappearance or a decrease in intensity of the systolic murmur is usual as flow across the right ventricular outflow tract diminishes. The onset is usually spontaneous and unpredictable. Spells are associated with reduction of an already compromised pulmonary blood flow, which, when prolonged, results in severe systemic hypoxia and metabolic acidosis. Infants who are only mildly cyanotic at rest are often more prone to the development of hypoxic spells because they have not acquired the homeostatic mechanisms to tolerate rapid lowering of arterial oxygen saturation, such as polycythemia.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":52,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-053","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"Depending on the frequency and severity of hypercyanotic attacks in tetralogy of Fallot, all the following procedures are true ExcEPT","o":["placement of the infant in the knee-chest position","administration of oxygen","injection of morphine subcutaneously","rapid correction with intravenous sodium bicarbonate irrespective of the spell severity","intravenous administration of propranolol"],"k":3,"ex":"Treatment: calm the infant, knee-chest position, oxygen, morphine 0.1 mg/kg SC/IM, IV fluid, IV propranolol 0.1 mg/kg slow, phenylephrine; sodium bicarbonate only for unusually severe spells not responding to the above (acidosis when PaO2 < 40 mmHg), then intubation/ventilation. Giving bicarbonate irrespective of severity is wrong.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Knee-chest position – increases SVR/pulmonary flow.\n\n- **B.** Oxygen – given.\n- **C.** Morphine SC – used.\n- **D.** Correct answer (EXCEPT) – bicarbonate regardless of severity is not appropriate (source answer D).\n- **E.** IV propranolol – used to relax infundibular spasm.\n\nSource explanation:\nBecause metabolic acidosis develops when arterial Po2 is <40 mm Hg, rapid correction (within several minutes) with intravenous administration of sodium bicarbonate is necessary if the spell is unusually severe and the child shows a lack of response to the foregoing therapy.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":53,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-054","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Investigation","q":"The typical radiologic configuration in tetralogy of Fallot as seen in the anteroposterior view consists of all the following ExCEPT","o":["normal heart size","some elevation of the cardiac apex","convexity in the region of the main pulmonary artery","right-sided aortic arch","diminished pulmonary vascularity"],"k":2,"ex":"TOF shows a boot-shaped heart (coeur en sabot): normal or small heart, uplifted apex from RV hypertrophy, CONCAVITY at the main pulmonary artery segment ('pulmonary bay'), diminished pulmonary vascularity and a right aortic arch in ~25%.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Normal heart size – typical.\n\n- **B.** Elevated apex – typical.\n- **C.** Correct answer (EXCEPT) – the pulmonary artery segment is concave, not convex (source answer C).\n- **D.** Right aortic arch – occurs in ~25%.\n- **E.** Diminished pulmonary vascularity – typical.\n\nSource explanation:\nConcavity in the region of the main pulmonary artery.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR: stray 'Y' line on page 344/345 boundary does not affect the item.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":54,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-055","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"The severity of symptoms of Ebstein anomaly and the degree of cyanosis are highly variable and depend on the extent of","o":["cardiac dysrhythmias","displacement of the tricuspid valve","atrial right-to-left shunt","pulmonary vascular resistance Y","polycythemia"],"k":1,"ex":"In Ebstein anomaly the tricuspid valve is displaced downwards, producing a huge right atrium and small functional RV, with right-to-left shunt across the PFO. Severity of symptoms and cyanosis depend on the extent of displacement of the tricuspid valve and the severity of right ventricular outflow obstruction (source explanation).","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Cardiac dysrhythmias – common complication (SVT/WPW) but not the main determinant.\n\n- **B.** Correct (source answer) – displacement of the tricuspid valve.\n- **C.** Atrial right-to-left shunt – a mechanism of cyanosis, but the extent of valve displacement is the keyed determinant.\n- **D.** Pulmonary vascular resistance – influences flow but not the keyed determinant.\n- **E.** Polycythaemia – a consequence of cyanosis.\n\nSource explanation:\nThe severity of symptoms and the degree of cyanosis are highly variable and depend on the extent of displacement of the tricuspid valve and the severity of right ventricular outflow tract obstruction.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR: the question number '55' is missing in the question text (stem begins 'The severity of symptoms of Ebstein anomaly…'); its number is taken from sequence position (between 54 and 56) and the answer key '55.(B)'. A stray 'Y' appears before option E.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":55,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-064","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"All patients with clinical evidence of a PDA are at increased risk for endocarditis. As result, PDA should be considered for closure EXCEPT","o":["PDA with severe irreversible pulmonary hypertension","small, hemodynamically insignificant PDA","small, hemodynamically significant PDA",".moderate size PDA","large PDA"],"k":0,"ex":"All PDAs with clinical evidence (audible) should be considered for closure because of endocarditis, heart-failure and pulmonary-hypertension risk – transcatheter closure for small, ligation for large. Exceptions are silent PDAs and PDAs with severe irreversible pulmonary hypertension (Eisenmenger physiology), where closure is contraindicated because the duct decompresses the right ventricle.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Correct answer (EXCEPT) – severe irreversible pulmonary hypertension (source answer A).\n\n- **B.** Small hemodynamically insignificant PDA – closure considered because of endocarditis risk.\n- **C.** Small hemodynamically significant PDA – closure indicated.\n- **D.** Moderate PDA – closure indicated (letter lost).\n- **E.** Large PDA – closure indicated.\n\nSource explanation:\nAll PDAs except for small silent PDAs and those patients with severe irreversible pulmonary hypertension should be considered for closure.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR: letter D lost; 'D.moderate size PDA' position assumed.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":64,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-065","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pharmacology","q":"Phenytoin is used for treatment of","o":["supraventricular tachycardia","atrial fibrillation","atrial flutter","ventricular tachycardia","digitalis intoxication"],"k":4,"ex":"Phenytoin (like lidocaine) is used for digitalis-induced ventricular and atrial arrhythmias. Supraventricular tachycardia, atrial fibrillation, atrial flutter and ventricular tachycardia are treated with other agents (e.g., adenosine, cardioversion, procainamide, amiodarone), as noted by the source.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** SVT – adenosine/cardioversion.\n\n- **B.** Atrial fibrillation – rate/rhythm control, cardioversion.\n- **C.** Atrial flutter – cardioversion.\n- **D.** Ventricular tachycardia – lidocaine, procainamide, amiodarone, cardioversion.\n- **E.** Correct (source answer) – digitalis intoxication.\n\nSource explanation:\nOther options are treated by procainamide.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":65,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-068","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"In urgent situations of supraventricular tachycardia (SVT) when symptoms of severe heart failure have already occurred. Of the following, the initial management is","o":["adenosine by rapid intravenous push","DC cardioversion (0.5-2 J/kg)","verapamil","digoxin","amiodarone"],"k":1,"ex":"In an unstable child (severe heart failure, shock) with SVT, synchronised DC cardioversion 0.5–2 J/kg is the initial treatment, repeatable up to three times, then amiodarone if ineffective. In a stable child, vagal manoeuvres followed by adenosine IV rapid push (0.1 mg/kg) are used.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Adenosine – first drug for STABLE SVT; may be tried if IV access is immediate.\n\n- **B.** Correct (source answer) – synchronised DC cardioversion.\n- **C.** Verapamil – avoid in infants.\n- **D.** Digoxin – slow; maintenance only.\n- **E.** Amiodarone – second line after failed cardioversion.\n\nSource explanation:\nIn urgent situations when symptoms of severe heart failure have already occurred, synchronized DC cardioversion (0.5-2 J/kg) is recommended as the initial management.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":68,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-073","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy (RCM) accounts for <5% of cardiomyopathy cases. RCM is characterized by all the following EXCEPT","o":["normal ventricular chamber dimensions","normal myocardial wall thickness","preserved systolic function","normal atrial chamber dimensions","high ventricular diastolic pressure"],"k":3,"ex":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy (<5% of cardiomyopathies) has a stiff, non-compliant myocardium: normal-sized or small ventricles with normal wall thickness and preserved systolic function, high ventricular diastolic pressure, and markedly dilated atria. Hence 'normal atrial chamber dimensions' is false.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Normal ventricular dimensions – true.\n\n- **B.** Normal wall thickness – true.\n- **C.** Preserved systolic function – true.\n- **D.** Correct answer (EXCEPT) – atria are dilated (source answer D).\n- **E.** High ventricular diastolic pressure – true.\n\nSource explanation:\nDramatic atrial dilation can occur as a result of the abnormal myocardial compliance and high ventricular diastolic pressure.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":73,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-078","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pharmacology","q":"Adverse reactions to angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors (ACEls) include the following EXCEPT","o":["hypotension","hypokalemia","maculopapular pruritic rash","renal toxicity","chronic cough"],"k":1,"ex":"ACE inhibitors cause hypotension (first dose), HYPERkalaemia, dry cough, rash, angioedema, neutropenia and renal impairment (bilateral renal-artery stenosis, hypovolaemia) and are teratogenic. Hypokalaemia is not an ACE-inhibitor effect.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Hypotension – occurs.\n\n- **B.** Correct answer (EXCEPT) – hypokalaemia (source answer B); hyperkalaemia is the effect.\n- **C.** Maculopapular pruritic rash – occurs.\n- **D.** Renal toxicity – occurs.\n- **E.** Chronic cough – occurs.\n\nSource explanation:\nAdverse reactions to ACEls include hypotension and its sequelae (weakness, dizziness, syncope) and hyperkalemia.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Curriculum discrepancy flagged: the curriculum text appears to contain an error ('hypo K'); the source answer is correct. The reviewer may wish to correct the curriculum notes.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":78,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-079","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (definition)","q":"The definition of hypertension in children is","o":["average systolic blood pressure (SBP) and/or diastolic BP that is ≥95th percentile for age, sex, on ≥3 occasions","average systolic blood pressure (SBP) and/or diastolic BP that is ≥95th percentile for age, sex, and height on ≥2 occasions","average systolic blood pressure (SBP) and/or diastolic BP that is ≥95th percentile for age, sex, and height on ≥3 occasions","average systolic blood pressure (SBP) and/or diastolic BP that is ≥90th percentile for age, sex, and height on ≥3 occasions","average systolic blood pressure (SBP) and/or diastolic BP that is ≥99th percentile for age, sex, and height on ≥3 occasions"],"k":2,"ex":"Hypertension in children is average systolic and/or diastolic BP at or above the 95th percentile for age, sex and height on at least three occasions (Fourth Report 2004; the 2017 AAP guideline keeps ≥ 95th percentile for ages 1–12 and uses ≥ 130/80 mmHg in those ≥ 13 years, replacing 'prehypertension' with 'elevated BP').","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Omits height – incorrect.\n\n- **B.** Only ≥2 occasions – incorrect.\n- **C.** Correct (source answer) – ≥95th percentile for age, sex and height on ≥3 occasions.\n- **D.** ≥90th percentile – this is prehypertension.\n- **E.** ≥99th percentile – stage 2 threshold.\n\nSource explanation:\nThe National High Blood Pressure Education Program Working Group on High Blood Pressure in Children and Adolescents published the Fourth Report on the Diagnosis, Evaluation, and Treatment of High Blood Pressure in Children and Adolescents (Fourth Report) in 2004. The Fourth Report defined hypertension as average systolic blood pressure (SBP) and/or diastolic BP that is z95th percentile for age, sex, and height on ≥3 occasions. Prehypertension was defined as average SBP or diastolic BP that are z9Oth percentile but <95th percentile.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Source quotes the Fourth Report (2004); the curriculum uses the newer AAP categories – the keyed definition remains valid.\n\n## SECTION 2 — EXTENDED ACCEPTED","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":79,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-001","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (syndrome–cardiac association)","q":"CHARGE association is characterized by the following cardiac feature","o":["endocardial cushion defect","supravalvular aortic stenosis","tetralogy of Fallot","patent ductus arteriosus","anomalous pulmonary venous return"],"k":2,"ex":"CHARGE (Coloboma, Heart defects, Atresia choanae, Retardation of growth/development, Genital hypoplasia, Ear anomalies; usually CHD7 mutation) carries a high rate of congenital heart disease. The classic cardiac pattern is conotruncal/aortic-arch malformations, with tetralogy of Fallot the keyed answer.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Endocardial cushion (AV septal) defect is the hallmark of Down syndrome. It is also reported in CHARGE, but it is not the keyed association (see Review Notes).\n\n- **B.** Supravalvular aortic stenosis is the hallmark of Williams syndrome.\n- **C.** Correct (source answer). Tetralogy of Fallot, with other conotruncal and aortic-arch anomalies, is the cardiac feature the source links to CHARGE.\n- **D.** PDA is non-specific (prematurity, congenital rubella, etc.) and is not the characteristic CHARGE lesion.\n- **E.** Anomalous pulmonary venous return is associated with heterotaxy/asplenia syndromes and the scimitar syndrome, not CHARGE.\n\nSource explanation:\nCHARGE association (coloboma, heart, atresia choanae, retardation, genital and ear anomalies) is associated with TOf, aortic arch and conotruncal anomalies.\n\nHigh-yield:\nCHARGE and DiGeorge share conotruncal lesions (TOF, truncus, interrupted arch).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Answer Verification (minor): AV septal defect is also described in CHARGE, so option A is not strictly impossible; the keyed answer follows the source's wording. No OCR problem.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":1,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-004","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Chest pain in the pediatric patient often generates a significant amount of patient and parental concern. Of the following, the MosT common cause is","o":["myocarditis","pericarditis","pleurisy",". pneumothorax","pneumonia"],"k":4,"ex":"Chest pain in children is overwhelmingly non-cardiac. The commonest causes are musculoskeletal (costochondritis, muscle strain, trauma), respiratory (asthma, cough, pneumonia), gastro-oesophageal reflux, anxiety/hyperventilation and idiopathic pain; true cardiac causes are uncommon.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Myocarditis – a cardiac cause; uncommon.\n\n- **B.** Pericarditis – cardiac cause; uncommon, pain is positional/pleuritic.\n- **C.** Pleurisy – is mostly a manifestation of an underlying pleural-irritating process (e.g., pneumonia) rather than an independent common cause.\n- **D.** Pneumothorax – uncommon (letter lost in OCR; position D).\n- **E.** Keyed answer (source answer E): pneumonia is the commonest of the listed conditions and is in the source's list of common causes.\n\nSource explanation:\nCommon causes are costochondritis, Trauma or muscle overuse/strain, asthma (often exercise-induced), severe cough, pneumonia, reflux esophagitis, anxiety, hyperventilation, precordial catch syndrome (Texidor's twinge), sickle cell vasoocclusive crisis, and idiopathic.\n\nHigh-yield:\nMost common overall: musculoskeletal/idiopathic (costochondritis); cardiac cause <5%.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Answer Verification Required (mild): the source explanation lists pneumonia among several common causes and does not rank them; the truly commonest causes (musculoskeletal/idiopathic) are not offered. Key accepted as best among options. OCR: letter 'D' missing before 'pneumothorax'.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":4,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-014","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Differential Diagnosis","q":"The following are causes of congestive heart failure and cardiomegaly during the newborn period but no murmur ExCEPT","o":["endocardial fibroelastosis","asphyxia","glycogen storage disease (Pompe disease)","paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia","sepsis"],"k":0,"ex":"Neonatal failure with cardiomegaly but no murmur suggests a myocardial or rhythm problem rather than a structural shunt lesion: perinatal asphyxia (ischaemic myocardial injury), Pompe disease, sustained SVT, sepsis, myocarditis. Endocardial fibroelastosis typically presents after the neonatal period (infancy), so it is the exception.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Correct answer (EXCEPT) – endocardial fibroelastosis presents after the neonatal period (source answer A).\n\n- **B.** Asphyxia – transient myocardial dysfunction.\n- **C.** Pompe disease (GSD II) – massive cardiomegaly, hypotonia.\n- **D.** Paroxysmal SVT – failure after prolonged tachycardia.\n- **E.** Sepsis – myocardial depression.\n\nSource explanation:\nEndocardial fibroelastosis is a cause after the neonatal period.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":14,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-019","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (epidemiology)","q":"One of the following cardiac lesions have been identified more in male","o":["transposition of the great arteries","atrial septal defect","VSD","PDA","pulmonic stenosis"],"k":0,"ex":"Transposition of the great arteries (and left-sided obstructive lesions such as AS and coarctation) is more frequent in boys (~65%). ASD, PDA and pulmonary stenosis are more frequent in girls.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Correct (source answer) – TGA, male predominance.\n\n- **B.** ASD – commoner in females.\n- **C.** VSD – the source lists it with the female-predominant lesions; the curriculum lists equal sex distribution (minor discrepancy).\n- **D.** PDA – commoner in females.\n- **E.** Pulmonic stenosis – commoner in females.\n\nSource explanation:\nGender differences in the occurrence of specific cardiac lesions have been identified. Transposition of the great arteries and left-sided obstructive lesions are slightly more common in boys (=65%), whereas atrial septal defect, VSD, PDA, anc pulmonic stenosis are more common in girls.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Discrepancy: source says VSD more common in girls; curriculum says equal sex. Does not affect the keyed answer.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":19,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-024","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"All the following are causes of chronic pulmonary venous hypertension ExcEPT","o":["congenital mitral stenosis","total anomalous pulmonary venous return with obstruction","peripheral pulmonary stenosis","left atrial myxomas","cor triatriatum"],"k":2,"ex":"Chronic pulmonary VENOUS hypertension arises from obstruction at or beyond the pulmonary veins/left atrium: congenital mitral stenosis, mitral insufficiency, obstructed TAPVR, left atrial myxoma, cor triatriatum, pulmonary vein stenosis and supravalvular mitral ring. Peripheral pulmonary stenosis is an obstruction on the arterial side, so it is not a cause.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Congenital mitral stenosis – cause.\n\n- **B.** Obstructed TAPVR – cause.\n- **C.** Correct answer (EXCEPT) – peripheral pulmonary stenosis is a pulmonary ARTERIAL lesion (source answer C).\n- **D.** Left atrial myxoma – can obstruct mitral inflow; cause.\n- **E.** Cor triatriatum – cause.\n\nSource explanation:\nA variety of lesions may give rise to chronic pulmonary venous hypertension, which when extreme may result in pulmonary arterial hypertension and right-sided heart failure. These lesions include congenital mitral stenosis, mitral insufficiency, total anomalous pulmonary venous return with obstruction, left atrial myxomas, cor triatriatum (stenosis of a common pulmonary vein), individual pulmonary vein stenosis, and supravalvular mitral rings.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":24,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-025","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (valvular disease)","q":"Regarding mitral valve prolapse; all the following are true ExcEPT","o":["it is predominantly congenital","it is usually sporadic","it is more common in girls","the dominant abnormal signs are auscultatory","antibiotic prophylaxis is recommended during surgery and dental procedures"],"k":4,"ex":"Mitral valve prolapse (MVP) is usually a sporadic, non-progressive congenital/myxomatous valve abnormality, commoner in girls, with auscultatory findings (mid-systolic click, late systolic murmur) as the dominant signs. Antibiotic prophylaxis is NO LONGER recommended for surgery or dental procedures in MVP (current AHA/ESC and NICE guidance), so the statement that it is recommended is false.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** 'Predominantly congenital' – accepted by the source as true.\n\n- **B.** 'Usually sporadic' – true.\n- **C.** More common in girls – true.\n- **D.** Dominant signs are auscultatory – true.\n- **E.** Correct answer (EXCEPT) – prophylaxis is no longer recommended (source answer E).\n\nSource explanation:\nThis lesion is not progressive in childhood, and specific therapy is not indicated. Antibiotic prophylaxis is no longer recommended during surgery and dental procedures.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Answer Verification (curriculum difference): the curriculum's UK-style table still recommends prophylaxis for 'moderate-risk' lesions including MVP with MR, whereas the source and current AHA guidance do not. The source answer is preserved; reviewer should be aware of this conflict.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":25,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-026","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (arrhythmia definition)","q":"Sinus bradycardia is due to slow discharge of impulses from the sinus node, the heart's natural pacemaker. Sinus bradycardia in neonates is considered if sinus rate is","o":["<100 beats/min","<90 beats/min","<80 beats/min","<70 beats/min","<60 beats/min 1 4"],"k":1,"ex":"Sinus bradycardia is a slow sinus-node discharge rate. The source defines it as < 90/min in neonates and < 60/min in older children.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** <100 beats/min – threshold used by PALS for infants; not the source's value.\n\n- **B.** Correct (source answer) – < 90/min in neonates.\n- **C.** <80/min.\n- **D.** <70/min.\n- **E.** <60/min – the source threshold for older children, not neonates.\n\nSource explanation:\nSinus rate <90 beats/min in neonates and <60 beats/min in older children is considered to be sinus bradycardia.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Answer Verification Required: the cut-off differs between references (<100 in PALS) and conflicts with the source's own answer to Q32 (lower limit of normal neonatal rate 70/min). Source answer preserved.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":26,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-032","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (normal values)","q":"The lower limit of pulse rate in neonate at rest is","o":["50/min","60/min","70/min","80/min","90/min"],"k":2,"ex":"The source quotes the normal neonatal heart-rate range as lower limit 70/min, average 125/min, upper limit 190/min. Values differ between references (PALS awake range roughly 100–205/min), so this is a source-specific figure.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** 50/min – below any accepted range.\n\n- **B.** 60/min – lower limit for older children.\n- **C.** Correct (source answer) – 70/min.\n- **D.** 80/min.\n- **E.** 90/min – the source's cut-off for neonatal sinus bradycardia (Q26).\n\nSource explanation:\nLower limit of normal is 70/min, average 125/min, and upper limit is 190/min.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Answer Verification Required: conflicts internally with Q26 (bradycardia <90/min in neonates) and with PALS thresholds; source answer preserved.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":32,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-034","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Investigation (ECG)","q":"Tall (>2.5 mm), narrow, and spiked P waves are seen in","o":["Ebstein anomaly","ventricular septal defect [VSD]","patent ductus arteriosus [PDA]","severe mitral stenosis","mitral regurgitation"],"k":0,"ex":"Tall (>2.5 mm), narrow, peaked P waves ('P pulmonale') indicate right atrial enlargement and occur in pulmonary stenosis, Ebstein anomaly, tricuspid atresia and cor pulmonale; they are best seen in leads II, V3R and V1. Broad, bifid P waves indicate left atrial enlargement (large left-to-right shunts such as VSD/PDA, mitral stenosis/regurgitation).","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Correct (source answer) – Ebstein anomaly (huge right atrium).\n\n- **B.** VSD – left atrial enlargement (broad P).\n- **C.** PDA – left atrial enlargement.\n- **D.** Severe mitral stenosis – broad, bifid P (left atrial enlargement).\n- **E.** Mitral regurgitation – broad P (left atrial enlargement).\n\nSource explanation:\nTall (>2.5 mm), narrow, and spiked P waves are indicative of right atrial enlargement and are seen in congenital pulmonary stenosis, Ebstein anomaly of the tricuspid valve, tricuspid atresia, and sometimes cor pulmonale. These abnormal waves are most obvious in leads Il, V3R, and V1. Similar waves are sometimes seen in thyrotoxicosis. Broad P waves, commonly bifid and sometimes biphasic, are indicative of left atrial enlargement. They are seen in some patients with large left-to-right shunts (ventricular septal defect [VSD], patent ductus arteriosus [PDA]) and with severe mitral stenosis or regurgitation. Flat P waves may be encountered in hyperkalemia.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Stray 'r' line at page break. Source explanation also notes similar P waves in thyrotoxicosis and flat P in hyperkalaemia.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":34,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-035","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"In utero heart failure, often with fetal pleural and pericardial effusions and generalized ascites (non-immune hydrops fetalis) may occur in","o":["ventricular septal defect","coarctation of aorta","Cd-Transposition of great arteries","Ebstein anomaly single ventricle"],"k":3,"ex":"The parallel fetal circulation compensates for most lesions, so most CHD does not cause fetal failure. Exceptions are severe regurgitant lesions, especially the tricuspid valve (Ebstein anomaly) or severe right outflow obstruction, where the right heart cannot handle the volume load – producing non-immune hydrops fetalis with pleural/pericardial effusions and ascites.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** VSD – well tolerated in utero.\n\n- **B.** Coarctation – usually tolerated before duct closure.\n- **C.** d-TGA – tolerated in fetal life.\n- **D.** Correct (source answer) – Ebstein anomaly with severe tricuspid regurgitation.\n- **E.** Single ventricle – usually no fetal failure unless valve regurgitation (letter lost).\n\nSource explanation:\nOne notable exception is the case of severe regurgitant lesions, most commonly of the tricuspid valve. In these lesions (e.g., Ebstein anomaly or severe right ventricular outflow obstruction, the parallel fetal circulation cannot compensate for the volume load imposed on the right side of the heart. In utero heart failure, often with fetal pleural and pericardial effusions and generalized ascites (nonimmune hydrops fetalis) may occur.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR: option letters lost for C ('Cd-Transposition') and E.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":35,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-038","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"The predominant manifestations of primary pulmonary hypertension include the following EXCEPT","o":["exercise intolerance","precordial chest pain","syncope","low arterial oxygen saturation","dizziness"],"k":3,"ex":"Idiopathic (formerly 'primary') pulmonary arterial hypertension presents with exercise intolerance, dyspnoea, fatigue, precordial chest pain, dizziness and syncope. Resting arterial oxygen saturation is usually normal unless there is an associated intracardiac right-to-left shunt (e.g., via a patent foramen ovale), so low saturation is not a predominant feature.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Exercise intolerance – predominant symptom.\n\n- **B.** Precordial chest pain – occurs (RV ischaemia).\n- **C.** Syncope – occurs (fixed cardiac output).\n- **D.** Correct answer (EXCEPT) – saturation is usually normal (source answer D).\n- **E.** Dizziness – occurs.\n\nSource explanation:\nArterial oxygen saturation is usually normal unless there is an associated intracardiac shunt.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"'Primary' pulmonary hypertension is now termed idiopathic PAH.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":38,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-039","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pharmacology","q":"The following are common side effects of sildenafil ExCEPT","o":["flushing","elevated liver function tests","headache","diarrhea","myalgia"],"k":1,"ex":"Sildenafil (phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitor) is used for pulmonary hypertension. Common adverse effects are flushing, headache, nasal congestion, dyspepsia/diarrhoea, myalgia/back pain and hypotension.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Flushing – common sildenafil effect.\n\n- **B.** Correct answer (EXCEPT) – elevated LFTs, not a typical sildenafil effect (source answer B).\n- **C.** Headache – common.\n- **D.** Diarrhoea – common.\n- **E.** Myalgia – recognised.\n\nSource explanation:\nFlushing, headache, diarrhea, hypotension, fluid retention, exacerbation of heart failure, anemia, elevated liver function tests, and palpitations are common side effects of bosentan.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Source discrepancy: the source explanation describes bosentan side effects rather than sildenafil; the keyed answer is still consistent with sildenafil pharmacology.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":39,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-043","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"For one of the following, total repair really achieved, with no requirement for long- term follow-up","o":["atrial septal defects","ventricular septal defects","valvar pulmonic stenosis","uncomplicated tetralogy of Fallot","isolated patent ductus arteriosis"],"k":4,"ex":"The source states that only for an isolated PDA is total repair really achieved, with no requirement for long-term follow-up after closure (with no residual shunt). ASD, VSD, valvar PS and repaired TOF carry late sequelae: arrhythmias/pulmonary hypertension, residual shunt, conduction block or aortic regurgitation, pulmonary regurgitation/restenosis and RV dilatation with arrhythmias.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** ASD – follow-up for arrhythmia/residual shunt, particularly if closed late.\n\n- **B.** VSD – risk of residual shunt, AV block, aortic regurgitation.\n- **C.** Valvar PS – pulmonary regurgitation/restenosis after valvuloplasty.\n- **D.** Uncomplicated TOF repair – lifelong follow-up (PR, RV dilatation, arrhythmia).\n- **E.** Correct (source answer) – isolated PDA.\n\nSource explanation:\nMany argue that only for isolated patent ductus arteriosus is total repair really achieved, with no requirement for long-term follow-up.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Answer Verification Required (mild): the source itself says 'many argue'; small ASDs closed in childhood and some closed VSDs may also need only minimal follow-up. Source answer preserved.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":43,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-046","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Complications","q":"The MosT common long-term complication for those patients who underwent early repair of complete atrioventricular canal before the development of pulmonary vascular disease is","o":["left AV valve regurgitation","subaortic stenosis","residual ventricular level shunts","complete heart block","endocarditis"],"k":0,"ex":"After early repair of complete AV canal (before pulmonary vascular disease), long-term prognosis is good. The commonest long-term complication is left AV (mitral) valve regurgitation, with about 5–10% needing valve repair or replacement.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Correct (source answer) – left AV valve regurgitation.\n\n- **B.** Subaortic stenosis – recognised but less common.\n- **C.** Residual ventricular-level shunt – uncommon.\n- **D.** Complete heart block – early/rare complication.\n- **E.** Endocarditis – rare.\n\nSource explanation:\nOverall, for those patients who underwent early repair before the development of pulmonary vascular disease, the long-term prognosis is good. The most common long-term complication is left Av valve regurgitation, with approximately 5-10% of patients requiring surgical revision for left Av valve repair or replacement during follow-up.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR: 'A.left' typo only.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":46,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-048","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (severity grading)","q":"Severe pulmonary valve stenosis defined as a peak gradient of","o":[">40 mm Hg",">45 mm Hg",">50 mm Hg",">55 mm Hg",">60 mm Hg"],"k":2,"ex":"Per the source, severe pulmonary stenosis is a peak gradient > 50 mmHg; most such patients need balloon valvuloplasty or surgery by age 25. Other references grade severe at higher thresholds (adult guidelines use a peak instantaneous gradient > 64 mmHg, velocity > 4 m/s), so cut-offs are reference-dependent.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** >40 mmHg – borderline/moderate in most schemes.\n\n- **B.** >45 mmHg.\n- **C.** Correct (source answer) – >50 mmHg.\n- **D.** >55 mmHg.\n- **E.** >60 mmHg – close to the adult-guideline severe threshold.\n\nSource explanation:\nFor those patients with severe stenosis (defined as a peak gradient of >50 mm Hg), the majority ultimately require an intervention, either surgery or balloon valvuloplasty by age 25 yr.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Answer Verification Required: thresholds differ between sources; source answer preserved.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":48,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-049","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Symptoms in patients with aortic stenosis depend on the severity of the obstruction. Critical aortic stenosisis associated with the following ExCEPT","o":["cardiomegaly","pulmonary edema","weak pulses in all extremities","pale skin","maximum intensity of the murmur"],"k":4,"ex":"Critical neonatal aortic stenosis presents with low cardiac output: cardiomegaly, pulmonary oedema, weak pulses in all extremities, pallor/poor perfusion and shock. With a markedly reduced cardiac output the murmur may be minimal, so 'maximum intensity of the murmur' does not fit.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Cardiomegaly – occurs.\n\n- **B.** Pulmonary oedema – occurs (LV failure).\n- **C.** Weak pulses in all extremities – occurs (low output).\n- **D.** Pale skin – occurs (poor perfusion).\n- **E.** Correct answer (EXCEPT) – murmur is softest, not maximal, when output is low (source answer E).\n\nSource explanation:\nIf cardiac output is significantly decreased, the intensity of the murmur at the right upper sternal border may be minimal.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR: 'stenosisis'; wording of option E is awkward but unchanged.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":49,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-051","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (complex lesions)","q":"Shone complex consist of the following ExCEPT","o":["coarctation of the aorta","subvalvar aortic stenosis","valvar aortic stenosis","pulmonic stenosis","mitral stenosis"],"k":3,"ex":"Shone complex is a combination of left-sided obstructive lesions: parachute or stenotic mitral valve (congenital mitral stenosis), supravalvar mitral ring, subvalvar/valvar aortic stenosis and coarctation of the aorta. Pulmonic stenosis is not part of it.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Coarctation – component.\n\n- **B.** Subvalvar AS – component.\n- **C.** Valvar AS – component.\n- **D.** Correct answer (EXCEPT) – pulmonic stenosis (source answer D).\n- **E.** Mitral stenosis – component.\n\nSource explanation:\nCongenital mitral stenosis is a rare anomaly that can be isolated or associated with other defects, the most common being subvalvar and valvar aortic stenosis and coarctation of the aorta (Shone complex).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":51,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-058","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Investigation","q":"In neonates with total anomalous pulmonary venous return and marked pulmonary venous obstruction, the chest x-ray demonstrates","o":["small heart","enlarged heart","prominent pulmonary artery","prominent right ventricle","subaortic stenosis"],"k":0,"ex":"In neonates with marked pulmonary venous obstruction (usually infracardiac TAPVR) the heart is of normal/small size with a striking perihilar (ground-glass) pulmonary oedema pattern. Unobstructed TAPVR shows cardiomegaly with plethora; the supracardiac type shows the 'snowman' (figure of eight) silhouette later in infancy.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Correct (source answer) – small heart.\n\n- **B.** Enlarged heart – unobstructed TAPVR.\n- **C.** Prominent pulmonary artery – with pulmonary hypertension, not the typical obstructed picture.\n- **D.** Prominent right ventricle – seen with volume overload.\n- **E.** Subaortic stenosis – not a radiographic finding.\n\nSource explanation:\nIn neonates with marked pulmonary venous obstruction, the chest x-ray demonstrates a very dramatic perihilar pattern of pulmonary edema and a small heart.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR: option E ('subaortic stenosis') reads oddly but is unchanged.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":58,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-059","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"In truncus arteriosus, a single arterial trunk arises from the heart and supplies the systemic, pulmonary, and coronary circulations. All the following are true ExCEPT","o":["VSD is always present","the truncus overriding the defect","both ventricles are at systemic pressure","heart failure ensues immediately after birth","clinical cyanosis is usually mild"],"k":3,"ex":"In truncus arteriosus a single arterial trunk, overriding a VSD, supplies the systemic, pulmonary and coronary circulations; both ventricles are at systemic pressure. Cyanosis is usually mild.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** VSD always present – true.\n\n- **B.** Truncus overrides the defect – true.\n- **C.** Both ventricles at systemic pressure – true.\n- **D.** Correct answer (EXCEPT) – failure develops over the first weeks as PVR falls (source answer D).\n- **E.** Cyanosis usually mild – true.\n\nSource explanation:\nWhen pulmonary vascular resistance is relatively highimmediately after birth, pulmonary blood flow may be normal; as pulmonary resistance drops in the 1st mo of life, blood flow to the lungs is greatly increased and heart failure ensues.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":59,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-060","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Clinical manifestations of hypoplastic left-heart syndrome include","o":["cyanosis always obvious in the 1st 48 hr of life","deep-blue color skin","bounding peripheral pulses","occasional association with Turner syndrome","autosomal recessive inheritance is usual"],"k":3,"ex":"HLHS presents in the first days with duct closure: grey-blue skin (mixture of cyanosis and poor perfusion, not obvious cyanosis in the first 48 h), weak or absent pulses, acidosis and collapse. It may occur in 5–15% of patients with genetic syndromes (Turner, trisomies 13/18/21, Jacobsen, Holt-Oram, Rubinstein-Taybi).","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Cyanosis always obvious in first 48 h – false.\n\n- **B.** Deep-blue skin – false (grey-blue).\n- **C.** Bounding pulses – false (weak or absent).\n- **D.** Correct (source answer) – occasional association with Turner syndrome.\n- **E.** AR inheritance usual – false (only occasionally familial).\n\nSource explanation:\nAlthough cyanosis may not always be obvious in the 1st 48 hr of life, a grayish- blue color of the skin is soon apparent and denotes a mix of cyanosis and poor perfusion. All of the peripheral pulses may be weak or absent. This lesion may be isolated or associated in 5-15% of patients with known genetic syndromes, such as Turner syndrome, trisomy 13, 18, or 21, Jacobsen syndrome (11q deletion), Holt-Oram syndrome, andRubinstein-Taybi syndrome. Occasionally it is familial and inherited as an autosomal recessive trait.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":60,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-063","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (epidemiology)","q":"The MOsT common cause of pulmonary hypertension in pediatric patients is","o":["idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension","pulmonary venoocclusive disease","left-sided valvular heart disease","chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","interstitial lung disease"],"k":0,"ex":"In paediatric pulmonary arterial hypertension registries, idiopathic or familial PAH is the commonest category (~55%), followed by PAH associated with congenital heart disease (~35%) and chronic respiratory disorders (~15%) per the source. Left-sided valve disease, COPD and interstitial lung disease are far less common causes in children.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Correct (source answer) – idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension.\n\n- **B.** Pulmonary veno-occlusive disease – rare.\n- **C.** Left-sided valvular disease – causes venous PH, uncommon in children.\n- **D.** COPD – adult disease.\n- **E.** Interstitial lung disease – uncommon.\n\nSource explanation:\nIdiopathic or familial disease is the most common in pediatric patients (~55%), followed by pulmonary hypertension secondary to congenital heart disease (~35%) and chronic respiratory disorders (~15%).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Answer Verification (mild): ranking depends on the classification used (PH due to CHD and neonatal lung disease is also very common clinically). Source answer preserved.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":63,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-069","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"Ventricular tachycardia (VT) is defined as at least 3 premature ventricular contractions PVCs at >120 beats/min. Hemodynamically unstable patients with VT should be immediately treated with","o":["catheter ablation","ICD implantation","DC cardioversion","amiodarone","procainamide"],"k":2,"ex":"Ventricular tachycardia with a pulse and haemodynamic instability is treated immediately with synchronised DC cardioversion; if pulseless, unsynchronised defibrillation and CPR. Drugs (amiodarone, procainamide, lidocaine) are for stable VT; catheter ablation and ICD implantation are definitive/long-term therapies.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Catheter ablation – elective, not emergency.\n\n- **B.** ICD implantation – long-term prevention.\n- **C.** Correct (source answer) – DC cardioversion.\n- **D.** Amiodarone – for stable VT or after failed shock.\n- **E.** Procainamide – stable VT.\n\nSource explanation:\nNone provided by the source (the key line gives the letter only).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Source key '69.(C)' has no explanation text; answer consistent with PALS.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":69,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-070","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Investigation","q":"In 90% of cases of endocarditis, the causative agent is recovered from the first 2 blood cultures. Antimicrobial pretreatment of the patient reduces the yield of blood cultures to","o":["10%","30%","40%","60%","80%"],"k":3,"ex":"In 90% of endocarditis cases the organism grows from the first two blood cultures. Antibiotic pretreatment reduces the yield to about 50–60%; the microbiology laboratory should be informed so that more sophisticated methods can be used.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** 10% – too low.\n\n- **B.** 30% – too low.\n- **C.** 40%.\n- **D.** Correct (source answer) – 60% (upper end of the source's 50–60%).\n- **E.** 80% – too high.\n\nSource explanation:\nAntimicrobial pretreatment of the patient reduces the yield of blood cultures to 50-60%. The microbiology laboratoryshould be notified if the patient has received antibiotics so thatmore sophisticated methods can be used to recover the offending agent.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"The source explanation states 50–60%; option D (60%) is the only matching option.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":70,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-071","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Complications","q":"Despite the use of antibiotic agents, mortality remains high, in the range of 20-25%. Serious morbidity occurs in 50-60% of children with documented infective endocarditis. Of the following, the MOsT common morbidity is","o":["heart failure","pulmonary emboli","mycotic aneurysms","acquired ventricular septal defect","heart block"],"k":0,"ex":"Mortality is 20–25% and serious morbidity occurs in 50–60%. The commonest complication is heart failure from valve destruction by vegetations (aortic or mitral); myocardial abscess and toxic myocarditis can also cause failure or arrhythmia.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Correct (source answer) – heart failure.\n\n- **B.** Pulmonary emboli – right-sided endocarditis.\n- **C.** Mycotic aneurysms – recognised but less common.\n- **D.** Acquired VSD – uncommon.\n- **E.** Heart block – uncommon (aortic root abscess).\n\nSource explanation:\nThe most common is heart failure caused by vegetations involving the aortic or mitral valve. Myocardial abscesses and toxic myocarditis may also lead to heart failure without characteristic changes in auscultatory findings and, occasionally, to life- threatening arrhythmias.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":71,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-076","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (tumours)","q":"The vast majority of tumors originating from the heart are benign. Of the following, the MosT common pediatric cardiac tumors are","o":["fibromas","rhabdomyomas","myxomas","hemangiomas","papillomas"],"k":3,"ex":"Rhabdomyomas are the commonest cardiac tumours in children and are associated with tuberous sclerosis in 70–95% of cases; they often regress spontaneously. Fibromas, myxomas, haemangiomas and papillomas are rarer.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Fibromas – second most common.\n\n- **B.** Correct – rhabdomyomas (the source key is OCR'd as '(8)'; the source explanation confirms rhabdomyomas).\n- **C.** Myxomas – commonest adult cardiac tumour, rare in children.\n- **D.** Haemangiomas – rare.\n- **E.** Papillomas – rare.\n\nSource explanation:\nRhabdomyomas are the most common pediatric cardiac tumors and are associated with tuberous sclerosis in 70-95% of cases.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR: the source answer reads '76.(8)' – a digit that is probably the letter B; preserved as 'Answer Verification Required (OCR)'. The explanation text names rhabdomyomas = option B.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":76,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-PEDIATRICS-077","spec":"cardio","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pharmacology","q":"Afterload reducers are not indicated in children with heart failure secondary to","o":["cardiomyopathy","severe mitral insufficiency","aortic insufficiency .ventricular septal defect","aortic stenosis"],"k":null,"ex":"Afterload reduction (ACE inhibitors, hydralazine) helps heart failure from cardiomyopathy, mitral or aortic regurgitation and left-to-right shunts (VSD). It is not generally used with stenotic lesions of the left ventricular outflow tract such as aortic stenosis, because vasodilatation lowers systemic pressure and coronary perfusion without relieving the fixed obstruction.","more":"Options analysis:\n- **A.** Cardiomyopathy – ACE inhibitors indicated.\n\n- **B.** Severe mitral insufficiency – indicated.\n- **C.** Aortic insufficiency – indicated.\n- **D.** VSD – indicated (letter lost).\n- **E.** Correct (source answer) – aortic stenosis.\n\nSource explanation:\nACEis and ARBs are not generally used in the presence of stenotic lesions of the left ventricular outflow tract because of concern over coronary perfusion.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Extended Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR: option D letter lost ('.ventricular septal defect').\nThe OCR question text/options or source key are incomplete; the answer is disabled until checked.","nlSource":"","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Cardiovascular System","nlNumber":77,"f":true,"kx":true},{"id":"NEL-RESPIRATOR-004","spec":"respiratory","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Investigation · **Depth:** Short","q":"Rigid bronchoscopy is preferentially indicated for A persistent pneumonia","o":["extracting foreign bodies","atelectasis","persistent wheeze `[label restored]`","interstitial disease"],"k":1,"ex":"The rigid bronchoscope gives a wide, secure airway channel and allows large instruments to pass, so it is both diagnostic and therapeutic. That makes it the tool of choice for removing foreign bodies (and, per Nelson, tissue masses and massive hemoptysis).","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nPersistent pneumonia, atelectasis, persistent wheeze and interstitial disease are all situations where the aim is to inspect the airway or sample secretions/tissue (diagnostic), which flexible bronchoscopy does well and with less risk. None of them needs the large-bore therapeutic channel of the rigid scope. Rigid bronchoscopy would only be considered for them if a therapeutic problem arises (for example, a retained plug or object).\n\nHigh-yield:\nForeign body = rigid bronchoscopy for removal; chest X-ray (inspiratory/expiratory) is the first imaging step.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.318 (printed 308) · Answer PDF p.330 · *Question number label lost in OCR; assigned by sequence and by matching the answer-key text \"Rigid bronchoscopy is preferentially indicated for…\".*","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Respiratory","nlNumber":4,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RESPIRATOR-009","spec":"respiratory","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (etiology / disease association) · **Depth:** Short","q":"Of the following, the MosT common childhood cause of nasal polyposis is","o":["chronic sinusitis",". allergic rhinitis `[label restored as B]`","Samter triad","Kartagener syndrome","cystic fibrosis"],"k":4,"ex":"Nasal polyps in a young child are unusual and point to cystic fibrosis. Thick, abnormal secretions and chronic sinonasal inflammation from the CFTR defect lead to polyp formation, and Nelson notes that as many as 30% of children with CF develop them.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nChronic sinusitis and allergic rhinitis are common but rarely cause polyps in children (allergic polyps are mainly an older-age problem). Samter triad (aspirin sensitivity, asthma, polyps) is a cause in adolescents and adults rather than the usual childhood cause. Kartagener syndrome (primary ciliary dyskinesia) can include nasal polyps, but PCD is much rarer than CF (about 1:20,000 versus 1:2,500).\n\nHigh-yield:\nChild with nasal polyps → do a sweat chloride test.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.319 (printed 309) · Answer PDF pp.330–331","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Respiratory","nlNumber":9,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RESPIRATOR-015","spec":"respiratory","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Differential Diagnosis · **Depth:** Moderate","q":"The barking cough typical of croup is rare in","o":["laryngotracheobronchitis","acute epiglottitis","acute infectious laryngitis","spasmodic croup","measles croup"],"k":1,"ex":"Acute epiglottitis (usually *H. influenzae* type b) is a bacterial supraglottic infection with abrupt onset, high fever, a toxic child, drooling and dysphagia, a muffled \"hot-potato\" voice, a soft stridor, and absent or slight cough. The inflammation is above the vocal cords, so the barking cough produced by subglottic swelling is characteristically missing.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nLaryngotracheobronchitis is viral croup itself, whose hallmark is a barking (brassy) cough with hoarseness and stridor. Acute infectious laryngitis and spasmodic croup sit in the same croup spectrum and also produce barking cough (spasmodic croup is recurrent, atopy-associated and usually without a viral prodrome). Measles croup is a croup-type infection of the larynx and trachea and also presents with barking cough; measles is listed among infectious causes of acute stridor.\n\nHigh-yield:\nCroup = barking cough, can drink, hoarse voice; epiglottitis = drooling, toxic, tripod, muffled voice. Never examine the throat with a tongue depressor in suspected epiglottitis.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.320 (printed 310) · Answer PDF p.331","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Respiratory","nlNumber":15,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RESPIRATOR-016","spec":"respiratory","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management · **Depth:** Moderate","q":"Children with croup should be hospitalized for any of the following ExCEPT","o":["progressive stridor","severe stridor at rest","cyanosis","depressed mental status","congenital heart disease"],"k":4,"ex":"Admission in croup is driven by how severe and how unstable the airway obstruction is. Nelson's list: progressive stridor, severe stridor at rest, respiratory distress, hypoxia, cyanosis, depressed mental status, poor oral intake, or need for reliable observation.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nProgressive stridor means worsening obstruction. Stridor at rest signals severe croup. Cyanosis reflects hypoxia and impending failure. Depressed mental status (or marked restlessness/dusky appearance in the curriculum's description) indicates hypoxia/exhaustion. All four mean the child needs inpatient observation and treatment (oxygen, steroid, nebulized adrenaline) and possible airway support.\n\nHigh-yield:\nAdrenaline gives rapid but transient relief, so observe 3–4 hours after it to avoid rebound.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF pp.320–321 (printed 310–311) · Answer PDF p.331","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Respiratory","nlNumber":16,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RESPIRATOR-017","spec":"respiratory","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (disease characteristics) · **Depth:** Moderate","q":"Bacterial tracheitis is characterized by all the following ExCEPT","o":["Staphylococcus aureus is the most commonly isolated pathogen","incidence and severity do not differ by sex","mean age is between 5 and 7 yr","considered a primary bacterial illness `[label restored as D]` more common than epiglottitis in vaccinated populations `[label restored as E]`"],"k":3,"ex":"Bacterial tracheitis (pseudomembranous croup) usually follows a viral respiratory infection, especially laryngotracheitis. It is therefore a bacterial complication of a viral illness rather than a primary bacterial illness, which makes D the false statement.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nThese are true statements. *S. aureus* is the most commonly isolated organism (the curriculum lists *S. aureus* and *S. pneumoniae*). Boys and girls are equally affected. The mean age in Nelson is 5–7 years (the curriculum gives a broader 3–10 years range). After Hib vaccination, epiglottitis became rare, and tracheitis is now the more frequently seen bacterial upper-airway obstruction. Options B and E rely on Nelson data beyond the lecture sheet, but D is identifiable from the taught viral-prodrome concept.\n\nHigh-yield:\nToxic-looking child with stridor and a barking-type cough who does not respond to croup treatment → think bacterial tracheitis.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.321 (printed 311) · Answer PDF p.331","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Respiratory","nlNumber":17,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RESPIRATOR-018","spec":"respiratory","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (etiology) · **Depth:** Short","q":"The MOsT common congenital laryngeal anomaly that produces stridor is","o":["vocal cord paralysis","laryngomalacia","congenital subglottic stenosis","congenital laryngeal web","congenital subglottic hemangioma"],"k":1,"ex":"Laryngomalacia (\"soft larynx\") is the most common cause of chronic congenital stridor in infancy. The supraglottic tissue collapses inward during inspiration, giving inspiratory stridor that starts at birth or within the first weeks, worsens with crying, feeding, URTI and lying supine, and improves when prone.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nVocal cord paralysis, subglottic stenosis, laryngeal web and subglottic hemangioma are all real causes of chronic stridor, but each is much less common. A web presents at birth and needs laryngoscopic ablation, subglottic stenosis gives biphasic stridor and is confirmed by bronchoscopy, and a hemangioma is a compression-from-inside lesion. They would be considered when stridor is atypical, progressive, biphasic, or accompanied by feeding or growth problems.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.321 (printed 311) · Answer PDF p.331","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Respiratory","nlNumber":18,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RESPIRATOR-026","spec":"respiratory","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (etiology) · **Depth:** Short","q":"The MosT frequent pathogens of pneumonia in children 5 yr and older is","o":["Streptococcus pneumoniae","Mycoplasma pneumoniae","group A streptococci","H. influenzae (type b, nontypable)","adenovirus"],"k":1,"ex":"In school-age children and adolescents, atypical bacteria dominate: *Mycoplasma pneumoniae* (and *Chlamydophila pneumoniae*) are the most frequent pathogens. Mycoplasma pneumonia typically begins gradually with cough and low-grade fever (\"walking pneumonia\"), with radiology often worse than the clinical picture, and may have extrapulmonary features (erythema multiforme/Stevens–Johnson, hemolytic anemia with cold agglutinins).","more":"","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF pp.322–323 (printed 312–313) · Answer PDF p.332","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Respiratory","nlNumber":26,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RESPIRATOR-027","spec":"respiratory","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (etiology) · **Depth:** Short","q":"The MOsT frequent pathogen of pneumonia in children 4 mo-4 yr is","o":["Streptococcus pneumoniae","Mycoplasma pneumoniae","group A streptococci","H. influenzae (type b, nontypable)","Respiratory syncytial virus"],"k":4,"ex":"In infants and young children, viruses cause most pneumonia, and RSV is the commonest (followed by parainfluenza, influenza and adenovirus). Bacteria such as *S. pneumoniae* follow in frequency.","more":"","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.323 (printed 313) · Answer PDF p.332","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Respiratory","nlNumber":27,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RESPIRATOR-028","spec":"respiratory","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (definition) · **Depth:** Short","q":"Recurrent pneumonia is defined as","o":["2 or more episodes in a single year, with radiographic clearing between occurrences","2 or more episodes in a single year, without radiographic clearing between occurrences","3 or more episodes in a single year, with radiographic clearing between occurrences","3 or more episodes in a single year, without radiographic clearing between occurrences","4 or more episodes in a single year"],"k":0,"ex":"Recurrent pneumonia means two or more episodes in one year (or three or more ever) with radiographic clearing of the densities between episodes. Clearing between episodes shows each one resolved, which is what separates it from persistent pneumonia.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nB and D describe no clearing between episodes, which indicates persistent or unresolved disease (suggesting a fixed lesion such as a foreign body, mass or bronchiectasis) rather than true recurrence. C and E set thresholds that do not match the accepted definition (the three-episode threshold applies over a lifetime, not within one year).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.323 (printed 313) · Answer PDF p.332","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Respiratory","nlNumber":28,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RESPIRATOR-029","spec":"respiratory","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis (clinical signs) · **Depth:** Short","q":"The MOsT consistent clinical manifestation of pneumonia is","o":["subcostal retractions","grunting","tachypnea","fever","cough"],"k":2,"ex":"Tachypnea is the most consistent and most sensitive sign of pneumonia, which is why respiratory rate must always be counted in a febrile child. Age-specific cut-offs apply (for example, above 50/min in infants and above 40/min at 1–5 years).","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nRetractions and grunting indicate increased work of breathing and more severe disease; they are common but absent in milder cases. Fever and cough are the commonest presenting symptoms, but they are not consistently present (for example, cough may be absent early or in young infants, and fever varies), and both are non-specific. Tachypnea is also non-specific on its own (it occurs in heart failure, metabolic acidosis and sepsis), but in a child with fever and respiratory symptoms it is the most reliable sign.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.323 (printed 313) · Answer PDF p.332","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Respiratory","nlNumber":29,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RESPIRATOR-031","spec":"respiratory","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (etiology) · **Depth:** Short–Moderate","q":"In the developed world, the Most common cause of clinically significant bronchiectasis is","o":["primary ciliary dyskinesia","foreign body aspiration","aspiration of gastric contents","immune deficiency syndromes","cystic fibrosis"],"k":4,"ex":"Cystic fibrosis is the commonest cause of clinically significant bronchiectasis in developed countries. Thick secretions and chronic infection (early *S. aureus*/Hib, later *Pseudomonas* and *B. cepacia*) destroy the airway walls, leading to bronchiectasis.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nAll of them are genuine causes. Primary ciliary dyskinesia is much rarer (about 1:20,000). A missed foreign body gives localized bronchiectasis. Recurrent aspiration of gastric contents can cause it in children with GERD or neuromuscular disease. Immune deficiency (such as hypogammaglobulinemia) predisposes to it through recurrent infection. Each becomes the leading consideration only when the history points to it (a sudden choking episode, neurologic impairment, recurrent severe infections with low immunoglobulins, or situs inversus with sinus disease).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF pp.323–324 (printed 313–314) · Answer PDF p.332","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Respiratory","nlNumber":31,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RESPIRATOR-032","spec":"respiratory","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis (clinical features) · **Depth:** Short","q":"The MosT common complaint in patients with bronchiectasis is","o":["cough and production of copious purulent sputum","hemoptysis","fever","anorexia","poor weight gain"],"k":0,"ex":"The typical bronchiectasis picture is a chronic productive cough with large volumes of purulent sputum (younger children may swallow it). Dilated, damaged airways pool infected mucus, so the cough is persistent and wet.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nHemoptysis occurs with some frequency but is less common than chronic wet cough, and can be severe in advanced disease. Fever appears mainly during infectious exacerbations. Anorexia and poor weight gain develop gradually with chronic illness.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.324 (printed 314) · Answer PDF p.332","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Respiratory","nlNumber":32,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RESPIRATOR-033","spec":"respiratory","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (clinical manifestations) · **Depth:** Moderate","q":"Cystic fibrosis (CF) can be presented with all the following EXCEPT","o":["salt depletion","pansinusitis","rectal polyposis `[label restored as C]`","pancreatitis","cholelithiasis"],"k":2,"ex":"CF is a multisystem disease from defective CFTR chloride transport. Nelson's list of manifestations includes exocrine pancreatic insufficiency, severe chronic lung disease, salt depletion, nasal polyposis, pansinusitis, rectal prolapse, pancreatitis, cholelithiasis, and insulin-dependent hyperglycemia, with failure to thrive and occasionally cirrhosis.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nSalt depletion (high sweat salt, salty-tasting skin, pseudo-Bartter syndrome with hypokalemia, hypochloremia and metabolic alkalosis) is typical. Pansinusitis and nasal polyps are typical upper-airway findings. Pancreatitis (in pancreatic-sufficient patients) and cholelithiasis are recognized but less common complications; the lecture sheet teaches pancreatic insufficiency and hepatobiliary disease/cirrhosis, so these two go slightly beyond the sheet but cannot be excluded as CF features.\n\nHigh-yield:\nRectal prolapse in a child → think CF.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.324 (printed 314) · Answer PDF p.332","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Respiratory","nlNumber":33,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RESPIRATOR-035","spec":"respiratory","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology · **Depth:** Moderate","q":"Arterial blood gas study in cystic fibrosis shows","o":["hypernatraemic hypochloraemic metabolic alkalosis","hyponatraemic hypochloraemic metabolic acidosis","hyponatraemic hyperchloraemic metabolic alkalosis","hyponatraemic hypochloraemic metabolic alkalosis","hypernatraemic hyperchloraemic metabolic acidosis"],"k":3,"ex":"Defective CFTR in the sweat duct prevents reabsorption of sodium and chloride, so sweat is salty and the child loses salt, especially in hot weather, fever or gastroenteritis. The result is hyponatremia and hypochloremia with volume contraction; hypokalemia and hypochloremia then drive a metabolic alkalosis.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nA and E state hypernatremia, but the sodium loss produces low, not high, sodium. B and E state metabolic acidosis, but the characteristic CF picture is alkalosis (acidosis would need another cause, such as sepsis or diabetic ketoacidosis). C and E state hyperchloremia, but chloride is lost with sodium, so chloride is low.\n\nHigh-yield:\nInfant with vomiting, dehydration and metabolic alkalosis with low Na/K/Cl → consider CF.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.324 (printed 314) · Answer PDF p.332 (key printed as \"35 (D).\" with no Nelson explanation in the OCR)","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Respiratory","nlNumber":35,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RESPIRATOR-040","spec":"respiratory","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Treatment · **Depth:** Moderate","q":"All the following inhalation therapies in cystic fibrosis are true ExcEPT","o":["β-agonists may decrease PaO acutely","human recombinant DNase improves pulmonary function","N-acetylcysteine is toxic to ciliated epithelium","nebulized hypertonic saline improves mucociliary clearance","aerosolized antibiotics are often used when the airways are colonized with S. aureus"],"k":4,"ex":"Aerosolized antibiotics in CF are used when airways are colonized with *Pseudomonas aeruginosa* (for example, tobramycin inhaled on a month-on, month-off schedule), which reduces symptoms, improves lung function and lowers hospitalization. Colonization with *S. aureus* is managed mainly with oral anti-staphylococcal antibiotics, so E is the false statement.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nRecombinant human DNase and hypertonic saline are standard mucolytic/airway-clearance therapies that reduce secretion viscosity, improve mucociliary clearance and lung function, and reduce exacerbations (curriculum: nebulized mucolytics such as DNase, hypertonic saline, acetylcysteine before physiotherapy). Option A is true according to Nelson (β-agonists can briefly worsen ventilation–perfusion matching and lower PaO2). Option C is also stated as true by Nelson (N-acetylcysteine can irritate and damage ciliated epithelium); note that the curriculum lists acetylcysteine as a mucolytic option, and the toxicity point in A and C goes beyond the lecture sheet.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.325 (printed 315) · Answer PDF p.333","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Respiratory","nlNumber":40,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RESPIRATOR-041","spec":"respiratory","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis (clinical features) · **Depth:** Moderate","q":"The following are clinical manifestations of primary ciliary dyskinesia (PCD) ExCEPT","o":["recurrent pneumonia","chronic otitis media","chronic pansinusitis","dextrocardia","retinitis pigmentosa"],"k":3,"ex":"The source key identifies option D (dextrocardia) as correct. Review the full explanation for the clinical reasoning.","more":"High-yield:\nSitus inversus + chronic sinusitis + bronchiectasis = Kartagener syndrome (PCD).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.325 (printed 315) · Answer PDF p.333","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Respiratory","nlNumber":41,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-010","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"The mainstay of treatment of phenylketonurea (PKU) is especial diet. All the following statements are true ExcEPT","o":["phenylalanine not totally restricted","all patients kept on a phenylalanine-restricted diet lifelong","especial diet should be used before and during pregnancy in mother with PKU","especial diet should be started when blood phenylalanine levels above 10 mg/dL","especial diet is not advocated in mild hyperphenylalaninemia whose levels are ≥ 6 mg/Dl"],"k":4,"ex":"In PKU a low-phenylalanine diet is the mainstay and is started when phenylalanine is persistently high (above about 6 mg/dL). The statement that the diet is not advocated in mild hyperphenylalaninemia at or above 6 mg/dL is therefore false.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** True: phenylalanine is an essential amino acid, so it is restricted, not eliminated (target level 2–6 mg/dL).\n\n- **B.** True: the diet is lifelong (curriculum).\n- **C.** True: maternal PKU is teratogenic (microcephaly, mental retardation, congenital heart disease), so the diet is needed before and during pregnancy.\n- **D.** True: treatment is started when levels are above 10 mg/dL.\n\nNelson explanation:\n10.(E). The mainstay of treatment of PKU is a low-phenylalanine diet. Most physicians\nadvocate phenylalanine-restricted diet 'in patients with mild hyperphenylalaninemia\nwhose levels are persistently above 6 mg/ dL (360 µmole/L). It is generally accepted\nthat infants with persistent (more than a few days) plasma levels of phenylalanine Z6\nmg/dl (360 umole/L) should be treated with a phenylalanine-restricted diet similar to\nthat for classic PKU.\n\nHigh-yield:\nPKU: AR, phenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency, mousy odour, diet for life; maternal PKU causes microcephaly and CHD.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Metabolic Disorders · question page 139 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":10,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-020","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"The MOsT appropriate treatment of homocystinuria is","o":["betaine","folic acid","vitamin C","vitamin B6","methionine restriction"],"k":3,"ex":"High-dose vitamin B6 (pyridoxine) is the most appropriate treatment of homocystinuria because many patients are B6-responsive (cystathionine synthase cofactor).","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Betaine lowers homocysteine and is an add-on, not the main therapy.\n\n- **B.** Folic acid is given with pyridoxine as support (curriculum), not as the main treatment.\n- **C.** Vitamin C has no established role.\n- **E.** Methionine restriction is used in B6-non-responders and as an adjunct.\n\nNelson explanation:\n20.(D). Treatment with high doses of vitamin B6 (200-1,000 mg/24 hr) causes dramatic\nimprovement in most patients who are responsive to this therapy.\n\nHigh-yield:\nHomocystinuria: B6 + folate + low-methionine diet; avoid dehydration (thromboembolism risk).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Metabolic Disorders · question page 141 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":20,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-021","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"The diagnosis of homocystinuria is usually made after 3 yr of age when the ophthalmologist found","o":["cataracts","glaucoma","astigmatism","ectopia lentis","retinal detachment"],"k":3,"ex":"Homocystinuria is usually diagnosed after 3 years when the ophthalmologist finds ectopia lentis (lens dislocation, typically downward and inward).","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Cataracts are not the typical lens finding.\n\n- **B.** Glaucoma can complicate dislocated lenses but is not the diagnostic finding.\n- **C.** Astigmatism is not specific.\n- **E.** Retinal detachment is not specific.\n\nNelson explanation:\n21.(D). Infants with homocystinuria are normal at birth. Clinical manifestations during\ninfancy are nonspecific and may include failure to thrive and developmental delay. The\ndiagnosis is usually made after 3 yr of age, when subluxation of the ocular lens (ectopia\nlentis) occurs.\n\nHigh-yield:\nHomocystinuria: marfanoid, ectopia lentis (down and in), thromboembolism, intellectual disability. Marfan: lens up and out.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Metabolic Disorders · question page 141 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":21,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-034","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"The definitive diagnosis of type I glycogen storage disease GSD is by","o":["EMG","liver biopsy","muscle biopsy","analysis of gene-based mutation","enzyme assay of cultured skin fibroblasts"],"k":1,"ex":"Historically the definitive diagnosis of type I GSD required liver biopsy with enzyme assay. The curriculum lists liver biopsy among the investigations, and the source key is liver biopsy.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** EMG is not used (muscle GSDs are assessed with exercise tests/biopsy).\n\n- **C.** Muscle biopsy is for muscle-type GSDs (e.g., McArdle), not type I.\n- **D.** Gene analysis is now the non-invasive way to diagnose types Ia and Ib (also in the curriculum list).\n- **E.** Enzyme assay in cultured fibroblasts is not used for glucose-6-phosphatase (a liver/kidney enzyme).\n\nNelson explanation:\n34.(B). The diagnosis of type I GSD is suspected on the basis of clinical presentation and\nthe laboratory findings of hypoglycemia, lactic acidosis, hyperuricemia, and\nhyperlipidemia. Neutropenia is noted in GSD Ib patients, typically before 1 yr of age.\nAdministration of glucagon or epinephrine results in little or no rise in blood glucose\nlevel, but the lactate level rises significantly. Before the glucose- 6-phosphatase and\nglucose-6-phosphate translocase genes were cloned, a definitive diagnosis required a\nliver biopsy. Gene-based mutation analysis provides a noninvasive way to diagnose\nmost patients with types la and Ib disease.\n\nHigh-yield:\nGSD I (von Gierke): fasting hypoglycemia, lactic acidosis, hyperuricemia, hyperlipidemia, hepatomegaly, doll-like face.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Answer Verification Required: Nelson's explanation says gene-based mutation analysis (D) is now the non-invasive way to diagnose types Ia/Ib, and the curriculum lists both liver biopsy and genetic study. Source answer (B) preserved. OCR note: options A and B appear merged in the transcription.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Metabolic Disorders · question page 144 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":34,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-039","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Irreversible complication of untreated galactosemia is","o":["sepsis","cataract","renal injury","myocardial damage","intellectual disability"],"k":4,"ex":"Intellectual disability (with cirrhosis) is the irreversible damage of untreated galactosemia. Other complications improve or are preventable with a galactose-free diet.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** E. coli sepsis is an acute, treatable complication.\n\n- **B.** Cataract regresses with early galactose restriction.\n- **C.** Renal tubular dysfunction (Fanconi) is reversible with dietary treatment.\n- **D.** Myocardial damage is not a feature of galactosemia.\n\nNelson explanation:\n39.(E). In galactosemia when the diagnosis is not made at birth, damage to the liver\n(cirrhosis) and brain (intellectual disability) becomes increasingly severe and\nirreversible.\n\nHigh-yield:\nGalactosemia: vomiting, jaundice, hepatomegaly, cataracts, E. coli sepsis after milk feeding; urine reducing substances; galactose-free diet.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Metabolic Disorders · question page 145 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":39,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-075","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"A 3-year-old boy presented to emergency unit at 10:00 AM with history of difficult arousal from sleep, lethargy, vomiting, and seizures; the mother stated that he had slept without having his supper. Fasting blood sugar was 38mg/dL and urinary ketone was low. Of the following, the MosT likely diagnosis is","o":["galactosemia","propionic acidemia","ketotic hypoglycemia","hereditary fructose intolerance","medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase (MCAD) deficiency"],"k":4,"ex":"Lethargy, seizures and hypoglycaemia after a prolonged fast with low urinary ketones (hypoketotic hypoglycaemia) is the picture of MCAD deficiency, a fatty-acid oxidation defect.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Galactosemia presents in neonates after milk feeding with jaundice, not fasting hypoglycaemia in a 3-year-old.\n\n- **B.** Propionic acidemia causes metabolic acidosis, hyperammonemia and ketosis.\n- **C.** Ketotic hypoglycaemia has high urinary ketones.\n- **D.** Hereditary fructose intolerance follows fructose/sucrose intake.\n\nNelson explanation:\n75.(E). MCAD; Previously undiagnosed affected patients usually present in the first\n3 mo-5 yr of life with episodes of acute illness triggered by prolonged fasting (longer\nthan 12-16 hr). During acute episodes of MCAD deficiency, hypoglycemia is usually\npresent. Plasma and urinary ketone concentrations are inappropriately low\n(hypoketotic hypoglycemia).\n\nHigh-yield:\nHypoglycaemia + low ketones after fasting = fatty-acid oxidation defect (MCAD). Treat with IV dextrose and avoid fasting.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Metabolic Disorders · question page 152 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":75,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-012","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"All the following are features of ataxia-telangiectasia ExcEPT","o":["hepatic manifestation","high incidence of malignancy","chronic sinopulmonary disease","oculocutaneous telangiectasias","variable humoral and cellular immunodeficiency"],"k":0,"ex":"Ataxia-telangiectasia is a DNA-repair disorder with cerebellar ataxia, telangiectasia, sinopulmonary infections, malignancy and combined immunodeficiency. Hepatic disease is not a feature.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **B.** Malignancy (lymphoma, brain tumours) is a feature.\n\n- **C.** Recurrent sinopulmonary infection is a feature.\n- **D.** Oculocutaneous telangiectasia is a feature.\n- **E.** Variable humoral and cellular immunodeficiency (combined B + T defect) is a feature.\n\nNelson explanation:\n12.(A). The most prominent clinical features are progressive cerebellar ataxia,\noculocutaneous telangiectasias, chronic sinopulmonary disease, a high incidence of\nmalignancy, and variable humoral and cellular immunodeficiency.\n\nHigh-yield:\nAtaxia-telangiectasia: AR, ataxia at about 2 years, telangiectasia at 3–6 years, raised AFP, malignancy risk.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Immunology · question page 195 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":12,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-001","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAlDs) are prescribed to decrease acute and chronic inflammation associated with various rheumatic diseases; however, many adverse effects have been encountered with long term use. Of the following, the NSAID that has the highest toxicity is","o":["celecoxib","meloxicam","indomethacin","naproxen","ibuprofen"],"k":2,"ex":"Indomethacin has the highest risk of toxicity among the listed NSAIDs, ibuprofen the lowest, naproxen intermediate, and COX-2 selective agents have fewer GI effects.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Celecoxib (COX-2 selective) has fewer GI adverse effects.\n\n- **B.** Meloxicam (preferential COX-2) has fewer GI adverse effects.\n- **D.** Naproxen carries intermediate toxicity.\n- **E.** Ibuprofen has the lowest toxicity of the non-selective agents.\n\nNelson explanation:\n1.(C). Certain agents (indomethacin) have a higher risk of toxicity than others\n(ibuprofen); naproxen has an intermediate risk. Selective COx-2 inhibitors (such as\ncelecoxib and meloxicam) inhibit receptors responsible for promoting inflammation\nwith potential for fewer gastrointestinal adverse effects.\n\nHigh-yield:\nNSAIDs relieve symptoms only; they do not modify the course of JIA.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Rheumatic Diseases of Childhood · question page 234 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":1,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-002","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 7-year-old boy developed small hypopigmented depressed scars after fingernail scratches; he has been treated with nonsteroidal antiinflammatory drugs (NSAiDs) for a rheumatic disease 6 weeks ago. Of the following, the NSAiD that is MOsT likely to cause such a unique skin reaction is","o":["celecoxib","meloxicam","indomethacin","naproxen","ibuprofen"],"k":3,"ex":"Naproxen is the NSAID most associated with pseudoporphyria: small depressed hypopigmented scars after minor trauma on sun-exposed skin in fair-skinned children.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Celecoxib is not typically linked to pseudoporphyria.\n\n- **B.** Meloxicam is not typically linked.\n- **C.** Indomethacin is associated with GI and CNS toxicity.\n- **E.** Ibuprofen is rarely implicated.\n\nNelson explanation:\n2.(D). Naproxen is more likely than other NSAlDs to cause pseudoporphyria.\nPseudoporphyria is more likely to occur in fair-skinned individuals and on sun-exposed\nareas. If pseudoporphyria develops; the inciting NSAlD should be discontinued because\nscars can persist for years or be permanent.\n\nHigh-yield:\nPseudoporphyria: NSAID (naproxen), sun exposure, blisters/scars; stop the drug.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Rheumatic Diseases of Childhood · question page 234 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":2,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-005","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"You are meeting parents of a 14-year-old girl who has been treated with a monthly intravenous cyclophosphamide for SLE-associated renal failure for the last 6 months; the mother is asking about the long-term complications of this drug. All the following are long-term complications ExCEPT","o":["bone marrow suppression","bladder cancer","leukemia","lymphoma","infertility"],"k":0,"ex":"Bone marrow suppression is a short-term adverse effect of cyclophosphamide. The long-term risks are bladder cancer, leukaemia, lymphoma and infertility.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **B.** Bladder cancer is a long-term risk.\n\n- **C.** Leukaemia is a long-term risk.\n- **D.** Lymphoma is a long-term risk.\n- **E.** Infertility (gonadal toxicity) is a long-term risk.\n\nNelson explanation:\n5.(A). Nausea, vomiting, anorexia, alopecia, mucositis, hemorrhagic cystitis, and bone\nmarrow suppression are potential short-term adverse effects.\n\nHigh-yield:\nCyclophosphamide: haemorrhagic cystitis (use mesna, hydration), marrow suppression, alopecia; long term: malignancy, infertility.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Rheumatic Diseases of Childhood · question page 235 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":5,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-006","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Juvenile idiopathic arthritis has many subtypes. Arthritis must be present to make a diagnosis of any subtype; involved joints oftenly have the following signs ExcEPT","o":["swelling","warm","erythema","limitation of movement","pain on movement"],"k":2,"ex":"In JIA the joint is swollen, warm, painful on movement and limited, but usually not red. Erythema suggests septic arthritis or another cause.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Swelling (effusion) is a key sign.\n\n- **B.** Warmth is present.\n- **D.** Limited range of movement is present.\n- **E.** Pain on movement is present.\n\nNelson explanation:\n6.(C). Involved joints are often swollen, warm to touch, and painful on movement or\npalpation with reduced range of motion, but usually are not erythematous.\n\nHigh-yield:\nJIA: arthritis for ≥6 weeks, age <16 years, other causes excluded; redness is absent.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Rheumatic Diseases of Childhood · question page 235 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":6,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-007","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Oligoarthritis, the most common subtype of juvenile idiopathic arthritis, is defined as involving ≤4 joints within the 1st 6 mo of disease onset. Of the following, the MosT commonly affected joint is","o":["hip","knee","elbow","wrist","metatarsal"],"k":1,"ex":"Oligoarticular JIA mostly affects large lower-limb joints, especially the knee, then the ankle.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** The hip is rarely involved at onset.\n\n- **C.** Isolated upper-limb large joint involvement is less common.\n- **D.** Wrist involvement is less common in oligoarthritis.\n- **E.** Metatarsal (small joint) involvement is uncommon in oligoarthritis.\n\nNelson explanation:\n7.(B). In oligoarthritis, it predominantly affects the large joints of the lower extremities,\nsuch as the knees and ankles. Isolated involvement of upper extremity large joints is\nless common.\n\nHigh-yield:\nOligoarthritis: girls, young age, ANA positive, chronic anterior uveitis (slit-lamp screening).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Rheumatic Diseases of Childhood · question page 235 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":7,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-010-2","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Investigation","q":"Antinuclear antigen (ANA) measurement test is useful in some rheumatologic diseases especially with persistent oligoarticular juvenile idiopathic rheumatoid arthritis (JIA). All the following are more likely to be correlated with ANA positivity ExCEPT","o":["anterior uveitis","younger age at disease onset","female sex","symmetrical arthritis","lower number of involved joints over time"],"k":3,"ex":"In oligoarticular JIA, ANA positivity correlates with asymmetric arthritis, not symmetric arthritis.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** ANA positivity goes with anterior uveitis and younger age at onset (OCR merged these two options).\n\n- **C.** Female sex is associated with ANA positivity.\n- **E.** A lower number of involved joints over time is associated with ANA positivity.\n\nNelson explanation:\n10.(D). ANA positivity is more likely to be correlated with asymmetric arthritis.\n\nHigh-yield:\nANA-positive JIA: young girl, oligoarthritis, asymmetric, uveitis; needs regular slit-lamp screening.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR note: options A and B appear merged in the transcription; the key (D) is unaffected.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Rheumatic Diseases of Childhood · question page 236 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":10,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-012-2","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 5-year-old boy develops acute onset of high spiking fevers, lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, and purpura; he has been diagnosed with systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis (JiA) since early childhood treated with antinflammatory drugs. You suspect macrophage activating syndrome (MAs). Of the following, the BEsT test that distinguishes MAS from a flare of the primary disease is","o":["leucopenia","falling ESR","hypofibrinogenemia","hypertriglyceridemia","evidence of hemophagocytosis in the bone marrow 228 [Continuation on source page 237]"],"k":4,"ex":"Bone marrow showing haemophagocytosis confirms macrophage activation syndrome (MAS) and separates it from a flare of systemic JIA.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Falling white cell count occurs in MAS but is not confirmatory.\n\n- **B.** A falling ESR (from fibrinogen drop) is a clue but not confirmatory.\n- **C.** Hypofibrinogenaemia is a supportive laboratory feature, not confirmatory.\n- **D.** Hypertriglyceridaemia is supportive, not confirmatory.\n\nNelson explanation:\n12.(E). The most important indicators of MAS include a falling platelet count, extreme\nhyperferritinemia, increased liver enzymes, falling leukocyte count, persistent,\ncontinuous fever Z38'C, falling ESR, hypofibrinogenemia, and hypertriglyceridemia. The\ndiagnosis is confirmed by bone marrow biopsy demonstrating hemophagocytosis.\n\nHigh-yield:\nMAS in sJIA: persistent fever, cytopenias, very high ferritin, low ESR/fibrinogen, high triglycerides, haemophagocytosis.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Rheumatic Diseases of Childhood · question page 236 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":12,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-013","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Rheumatoid factor (RF)-positive polyarthritis usually accounts for < 10% of all juvenile idiopathic arthritis (JiA) cases. The articular manifestation pattern is characterized by involvement of 25 joints in both upper and lower extremities. Of the following, the LEAsT effective drug to induce remission for thls subtype of JIA is","o":["NSAIDS","methotrexate","TNF-α antagonists","IL-1 inhibitors","IL-6 inhibitors"],"k":0,"ex":"NSAIDs only relieve symptoms and do not modify the disease, so they rarely induce remission in RF-positive polyarticular JIA. Methotrexate and biologics control the disease.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **B.** Methotrexate is effective in about 70% of polyarthritis and reduces joint damage when started early.\n\n- **C.** TNF-α antagonists give excellent disease control.\n- **D.** IL-1 inhibitors (e.g., anakinra) are effective biologics (mainly systemic JIA).\n- **E.** IL-6 inhibitors (e.g., tocilizumab) are effective biologics.\n\nNelson explanation:\n13.(A). NSAlDs alone rarely induces remission in children with polyarthritis or systemic\nJiA. Methotrexate is the oldest and least toxic effective drug. Biologic medications that\n237\n[Source page 248]\ninhibit proinflammatory cytokines, such as TNF-a, IL-1, and IL-6, demonstrated\nexcellent disease control.\n\nHigh-yield:\nJIA: NSAID/analgesic = symptomatic; methotrexate = first DMARD; then biologics.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Rheumatic Diseases of Childhood · question page 237 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":13,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-015","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"You are discussing with the medical students the role of systemic steroids for the management of rheumnatologic diseases. Your discussion should include all the following statements ExcEPT","o":["they are recommended for management of severe systemic illness","they are used for bridge therapy during the wait for therapeutic response to a disease modified anti rheumatic drugs (DMARD)","they are effective for control of uveitis","they prevent joint destruction","they impose risks of severe toxicities"],"k":3,"ex":"Systemic steroids control severe systemic illness and uveitis and bridge the wait for DMARDs, but they do not prevent joint destruction.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** True: used in severe systemic illness.\n\n- **B.** True: bridging therapy until DMARDs work.\n- **C.** True: effective for uveitis.\n- **E.** True: they carry significant toxicity (growth failure, osteoporosis, etc.).\n\nNelson explanation:\n15.(D). Systemic steroids do not prevent joint destruction.\n\nHigh-yield:\nIn JIA, steroids are avoided if possible; IV steroids are used for severe polyarthritis, systemic JIA or MAS.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Rheumatic Diseases of Childhood · question page 237 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":15,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-016","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Chronic uveitis is one of the extraarticular manifestations of various subtypes of juvenile idiopathic arthritis. All the following are risk factors for the development of uveitis ExcEPT","o":["oligoarthritis subtype","female gender","ANA-positivity","onset of arthritis earlier than 6 yr of age","severity of arthritis"],"k":4,"ex":"Risk factors for JIA uveitis are oligoarthritis, female sex, ANA positivity and onset before 6 years. The severity or activity of the arthritis is not linked to uveitis.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Oligoarticular JIA carries the highest uveitis risk (about 20% in the curriculum).\n\n- **B.** Female gender is a risk factor.\n- **C.** ANA positivity and onset before 6 years are risk factors (OCR merged this option with the next one).\n\nNelson explanation:\n16.(E). There is no association between the activity or severity of arthritis and uveitis.\n\nHigh-yield:\nChronic anterior uveitis is often asymptomatic: screen with a slit lamp (ANA-positive oligoarthritis).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR note: options C and D appear merged in the transcription; the key (E) is unaffected.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Rheumatic Diseases of Childhood · question page 237 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":16,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-017","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"The child with polyarticular JlA often has a more prolonged course of active joint inflammation and requires early and aggressive therapy. Of the following, the predictor that carries the wOrsT prognosis is 227 [Continuation on source page 238]","o":["old age at onset","rheumatoid factor (RF) seronegativity","absence of rheumatoid nodules","small numbers of affected joints","hip joint involvement"],"k":4,"ex":"Hip involvement (and hand/wrist disease) in polyarticular JIA is a marker of poor functional outcome.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Per the source this factor does not carry a poor outcome.\n\n- **B.** RF seronegativity carries a better prognosis than RF positivity.\n- **C.** Absence of rheumatoid nodules is a good prognostic sign.\n- **D.** A small number of affected joints is a good prognostic sign.\n\nNelson explanation:\n17.(E). Disease involving the hip and hand and wrist is associated with a poorer\nprognosis and may lead to significant functional impairment. All other mentioned\nfactors carry a good outcome.\n\nHigh-yield:\nPoor prognosis in polyarticular JIA: RF-positive, hip/wrist/hand disease, nodules, erosions.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Rheumatic Diseases of Childhood · question page 237 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":17,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-018","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Spondyloarthritis may be overlapped clinically with other forms of juvenile idiopathic arthritis (JIA). Clinical manifestations that help to distinguish spondyloarthritis from other forms of juvenile arthritis include all the following ExCEPT","o":["arthritis of the sacroiliac joints","arthritis of the hips","enthesitis","asymptomatic uveitis","gastrointestinal inflammation"],"k":3,"ex":"Spondyloarthritis is distinguished by sacroiliac and hip arthritis, enthesitis, gut inflammation and acute symptomatic anterior uveitis. Chronic asymptomatic uveitis is the typical pattern of oligoarticular JIA.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Sacroiliac arthritis is a feature.\n\n- **B.** Hip arthritis is a feature.\n- **C.** Enthesitis is a feature.\n- **E.** GI inflammation (IBD association) is a feature.\n\nNelson explanation:\n18.(D). Symptomatic, rather than asymptomatic, eye inflammation (acute anterior\nuveitis) is a clinical feature suggestive of spondyloarthritis.\n\nHigh-yield:\nEnthesitis-related arthritis: boys, HLA-B27, lower-limb large joints, sacroiliitis, acute painful red-eye uveitis.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Rheumatic Diseases of Childhood · question page 238 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":18,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-019","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Enthesitis-Related Arthritis (ERA) is a subtype of rheumatic disease that falls in the juvenile idiopathic arthritis (JlA) category. Of the following, the clinical feature that is highly suggestive of ERA is","o":["symmetrical arthritis early in the disease course","early involvement of more than 5 joints","involvement of upper limb joints","Inflammation of the small joints of the foot","asymmetrical enthesitis"],"k":3,"ex":"Tarsitis (inflammation of the small joints of the foot) is highly suggestive of enthesitis-related arthritis.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** ERA is typically asymmetric, not symmetric.\n\n- **B.** ERA involves 4 or fewer joints in the first 6 months.\n- **C.** ERA mainly affects the lower limbs.\n- **E.** Enthesitis in ERA is typically symmetric, not asymmetric.\n\nNelson explanation:\n19.(D). In ERA, the arthritis is typically asymmetric and involves 4 or fewer joints during\nthe 1\" 6mo of the disease. The most frequently affected joints are the knees, ankles,\nand hips. Enthesitis is typically symmetric and most commonly affects the lower limbs.\nInflammation of the small joints of the foot, or tarsitis, is highly suggestive of ERA.\n\nHigh-yield:\nERA: HLA-B27, enthesitis (Achilles, plantar fascia), tarsitis, sacroiliitis.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Rheumatic Diseases of Childhood · question page 238 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":19,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-026","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Investigation","q":"A 4-year-old boy has an acute severe left groin pain that refers to thigh. Examination reveals limitation of movement of the left hip joint. Lab investigations reveal normal blood count and indices, and normal ESR; radiologic imaging shows widening of the hip joint space with effusion. You suspect transient synovitis. Of the following, the BEsT therapeutic approach for this boy is","o":["systemic steroids 229 [Continuation on source page 240]","methotrexate","aspiration of joint fluid","intravenous antibiotics","observation"],"k":2,"ex":"The source recommends joint aspiration, which excludes septic arthritis and often gives dramatic relief. In the curriculum a well-looking child with normal CRP/WBC and a transient synovitis picture is managed with analgesia and rest (self-limiting).","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Systemic steroids are not used in transient synovitis.\n\n- **B.** Methotrexate is a JIA drug, not used here.\n- **D.** IV antibiotics are for septic arthritis (ill, high CRP/WBC, fever).\n- **E.** Observation with analgesia and bed rest is the curriculum's treatment for typical transient synovitis.\n\nNelson explanation:\n26.(C). in transient synovitis (toxic synovitis), aspiration of joint fluid is often necessary\nto exclude septic arthritis and typically results in dramatic clinical improvement.\n\nHigh-yield:\nTransient synovitis: well child, mild/no fever, normal CRP/WBC, post-URTI, self-limiting. Septic arthritis: toxic, high CRP/WBC, cannot bear weight.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Answer Verification Required: the source key (C, aspiration) conflicts with the curriculum, which treats typical transient synovitis with analgesics and rest (option E). Source answer preserved.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Rheumatic Diseases of Childhood · question page 239 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":26,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-028","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is a chronic autoimmune disease characterized by multisystem inflammation and the presence of circulating autoantibodies directed against self-antigens. It occurs in both children and adults. All the following are features of childhood SLE ExCEPT","o":["it is has a more severe course","there is more widespread organ involvement","it usually presents before S year of age","fever, fatigue, hematologic abnormalities, and arthritis are common clinical manifestations","although renal disease is asymptomatic, it is oftenly present as nephrotic syndrome in adolescent age group"],"k":2,"ex":"Childhood SLE is rare before 5 years of age; it usually presents in adolescence (the curriculum gives age >5 years).","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** True: childhood SLE has a more severe course.\n\n- **B.** True: organ involvement is more widespread.\n- **D.** True: fever, fatigue, haematological abnormalities and arthritis are common.\n- **E.** True: renal disease is often clinically silent and may present as nephrotic syndrome in adolescents.\n\nNelson explanation:\n28.(C). Childhood SLE is rare before 5 yr of age.\n\nHigh-yield:\nSLE: 4 of 11 criteria; ANA most sensitive, anti-dsDNA and anti-Sm most specific; low C3/C4.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Rheumatic Diseases of Childhood · question page 240 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":28,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-029","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Skin is a commonly involved organ by SLE. There are different cutaneous manifestations. Of the following, the skin manifestation that is MOsT suggestive of SLE in children is","o":["malar rash","discoid rash","photosensitive rash","cutaneous vasculitis","livedo reticularis"],"k":1,"ex":"The source keeps discoid rash as the answer. All the listed rashes occur in SLE and the curriculum names malar and discoid rash among the 11 criteria without ranking them.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Malar rash is the classic SLE rash (butterfly, sparing the nasolabial folds).\n\n- **C.** Photosensitivity is a criterion but is non-specific.\n- **D.** Cutaneous vasculitis is seen but is less specific.\n- **E.** Livedo reticularis is non-specific (also in antiphospholipid syndrome).\n\nNelson explanation:\n29.(B).\n\nHigh-yield:\nMalar rash spares the nasolabial folds; discoid lesions scar and are highly specific.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Answer Verification Required: the source gives no explanation for the key (B), and the curriculum does not rank malar versus discoid rash. Source answer preserved.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Rheumatic Diseases of Childhood · question page 240 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":29,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-032","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"A 12-year-old female adolescent has a recent diagnosis of SlE; she has a faint malar rash and mild arthritis including both elbow and knee joints as well as the joints of small fingers. Of the following, the MosT appropriate initial therapy for this patient is","o":["systemic steroids","methotrexate","leflunomide","cyclophosphamide","hydroxychloroquine"],"k":4,"ex":"Hydroxychloroquine is recommended for every patient with SLE and is the first treatment for mild disease (rash, mild arthritis); it also prevents flares.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Systemic steroids are used for moderate to severe disease or flares, not as the sole first step in mild disease.\n\n- **B.** Methotrexate is an alternative for arthritis but not first line.\n- **C.** Leflunomide is not standard in SLE.\n- **D.** Cyclophosphamide is for severe organ disease (e.g., lupus nephritis).\n\nNelson explanation:\n32.(E). Hydroxychloroquine is recommended for all individuals with SLE if tolerated. In\naddition to treating mild SLE manifestations such as rash and mild arthritis,\nhydroxychloroquine prevents SLE flares, improves lipid profiles, and may have a\nbeneficial impact on mortality and renal outcomes. Corticosteroids are a mainstay for\ntreatment of significant manifestations of SLE. Methotrexate, leflunomide, and\nazathioprine are often used to treat persistent moderate disease, Including arthritis,\nsignificant cutaneous or hematologic involvement, and pleural disease.\nCyclophosphamide is reserved for the most severe, potentially life threatening SLE\nmanifestations, such as renal, neurologic, and cardiopulmonary disease.\n\nHigh-yield:\nSLE: hydroxychloroquine for all (eye screening yearly), sunscreen; steroids/cyclophosphamide for severe organ disease.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Answer Verification Required: the curriculum does not mention hydroxychloroquine; the key (E) rests on Nelson. Source answer preserved.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Rheumatic Diseases of Childhood · question page 241 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":32,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-040","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"You are discussing Raynaud phenomenon (RP) and Raynaud disease (RD) with medical students. You state that RP is usually associated with rheumatic diseases, while RD is independent of an underlying rheumatic disease. Of the following, the feature that is MOsT likely consistent with RD, rather than RP is","o":["presence in early childhood","absence of tissue necrosis and gangrene","asymmetric occurrence","presence of periungual telangiectasia","associated pain and paresthesia"],"k":1,"ex":"Raynaud disease (primary) shows no tissue necrosis or gangrene, begins in adolescence, is symmetric and has no underlying rheumatic disease. Raynaud phenomenon (secondary) may cause necrosis.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** RD begins in adolescence, not early childhood.\n\n- **C.** RD is symmetric; asymmetry suggests RP.\n- **D.** Periungual telangiectasia / abnormal nail-fold capillaries point to a rheumatic disease (RP).\n- **E.** Pain and paresthesia indicate ischaemic change, seen with RP.\n\nNelson explanation:\n40.(B). RD often begins in adolescence and is characterized by symmetric occurrence,\nthe absence of tissue necrosis and gangrene, and the lack of manifestations of an\nunderlying rheumatic disease. Children have normal nail-fold capillaries. Pain and\nparesthesia indicate ischemic changes, associated only with RP due to rheumatic\ndiseases.\n\nHigh-yield:\nSecondary Raynaud: abnormal nail-fold capillaries, ANA positive, digital ulcers.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Rheumatic Diseases of Childhood · question page 243 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":40,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-046","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Amyloidosis is a disease caused by protein misfolding; the type referred as amyloid A (AA), amyloidosis usually develops in patients with chronic inflammatory states, especially juvenile idiopathic arthritis. In which of the following subtypes of JIA there is a highest prevalence of AA amyloidosis?","o":["systemic JIA",". oligoarticular JIA","polyarticular JIA","psoriatic arthritis","enthesitis-related arthritis"],"k":0,"ex":"AA amyloidosis is most common in systemic JIA, followed by polyarticular disease.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **C.** Polyarticular JIA is second, after systemic JIA.\n\n- **D.** Psoriatic arthritis is rare.\n- **E.** Enthesitis-related arthritis is rare.\n\nNelson explanation:\n46.(A). JIA is a rheumatic disease that is associated with the development of AA\namyloidosis with the highest prevalence in patients with systemic JiA followed by those\nwith polyarticular disease.\n\nHigh-yield:\nAmyloidosis in JIA: proteinuria, renal failure; the most serious JIA complication is MAS.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR note: options A and B are merged in the transcription (oligoarticular JIA is the other subtype listed; it rarely develops amyloidosis). The key (A) is unaffected.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Rheumatic Diseases of Childhood · question page 244 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":46,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-049","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Kawasaki disease (KD) has unique clinical manifestations; however, less consistent clinical presentation may occur. All the following are common classic clinical features of KD ExcEPT","o":["bilateral non exudative bulbar conjunctivitis","cracked lips","edema and erythema of the hands and feet","vesicular rash","unilateral non suppurative cervical lymphadenopathy"],"k":3,"ex":"Kawasaki disease causes a polymorphous rash (maculopapular, erythroderma, erythema multiforme-like) but not a vesicular rash.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Bilateral non-exudative bulbar conjunctival injection is a criterion.\n\n- **B.** Red cracked lips/strawberry tongue is a criterion.\n- **C.** Oedema and erythema of the hands and feet is a criterion.\n- **E.** Cervical lymphadenopathy ≥1.5 cm, usually unilateral, is a criterion.\n\nNelson explanation:\n49.(D). Typical rash is maculopapular, erythema multiforme, or scarlatiniform. Bullous,\npustular, or vesicular rashes are uncommon skin manifestations.\n\nHigh-yield:\nKawasaki: fever ≥5 days + 4 of 5 criteria; give IVIG and aspirin; echocardiography for coronary aneurysm.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Rheumatic Diseases of Childhood · question page 245 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":49,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-052","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Henoch-Schonleln purpura (HSp) Is the most common vasculitis of childhood affecting small vessels in the skin, joints, gastrointestinal tract, and kidney. Gastrointestinal involvements occur in up to 80% of children with HSp. Of the following, the LEAsT gastrolntestinal manifestation that may occur in children with HSP Is","o":["abdominal pain","vomiting and diarrhea",".paralytic ileus","melena","Intussusceptlon"],"k":4,"ex":"In HSP, abdominal pain and vomiting/diarrhoea are common; intussusception, mesenteric ischaemia and perforation are uncommon.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Abdominal pain is the commonest GI feature.\n\n- **B.** Vomiting and diarrhoea are common (OCR merged with the next option).\n- **D.** Melena/GI bleeding is a recognised manifestation.\n\nNelson explanation:\n52.(E). Intussusception, mesenteric ischemia, and intestinal perforation are uncommon\nin children with HSP.\n\nHigh-yield:\nHSP: palpable purpura on legs/buttocks, arthritis, colicky abdominal pain, nephritis; the commonest childhood vasculitis.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR note: options B and C appear merged (\"vomiting and diarrhea . paralytic ileus\"); the key (E) matches Nelson's explanation.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Rheumatic Diseases of Childhood · question page 246 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":52,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-053","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"A S-year-old boy has acute onset of abdominal pain and melena; he has been diagnosed with Henoch-Schönlein purpura (HSP) one week before. Examination reveals symmetrical palpable purpura overlying the lower extremities and buttock; other examinations are unremarkable. Of the following, the BEsT treatment for this boy is","o":["supportive measures",". steroids","azathioprine","cyclophosphamide","mycophenolate mofetil"],"k":1,"ex":"Steroids are used in HSP when there is significant gastrointestinal involvement (severe abdominal pain, melena) or another serious manifestation; mild disease needs supportive care only.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Supportive care alone is for mild disease; melena indicates significant GI involvement (OCR merged options A and B).\n\n- **C.** Azathioprine is an immunosuppressant for chronic HSP nephritis.\n- **D.** Cyclophosphamide is for severe/crescentic nephritis.\n- **E.** Mycophenolate mofetil is for chronic HSP nephritis.\n\nNelson explanation:\n53.(B). Steroid is most often used to treat significant gastrointestinal involvement or\nother life-threatening manifestations.\n\nHigh-yield:\nHSP: mostly supportive; steroids for severe abdominal pain/GI bleeding, testicular or CNS involvement; monitor urine for 6 months.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR note: options A and B appear merged (\"supportive measures . steroids\"); the key (B) corresponds to steroids per Nelson's explanation.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Rheumatic Diseases of Childhood · question page 246 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":53,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-054","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"Renal disease is the major long-term complication, occurring in 1-2% of children with Henoch-Schönlein purpura (HSP). Chronic HSP renal disease is managed with a variety of immunosuppressants. Of the following, the LEAsT effective drug for the treatment of renal disease in HSP is","o":["prednisolone","azathioprine","cyclophosphamide","cyclosporine","mycophenolate mofetil"],"k":0,"ex":"Prednisolone is the least effective drug listed for chronic HSP renal disease. Azathioprine, cyclophosphamide, cyclosporine and mycophenolate mofetil are the immunosuppressants used.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **B.** Azathioprine is used for chronic HSP nephritis.\n\n- **C.** Cyclophosphamide is used for severe nephritis.\n- **D.** Cyclosporine is used in refractory nephritis.\n- **E.** Mycophenolate mofetil is used for chronic nephritis.\n\nNelson explanation:\n54.(A).\n\nHigh-yield:\nRenal disease occurs in 1–2% of HSP and is the main long-term complication.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"The source gives no explanation for this key (A); it is kept as provided.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Rheumatic Diseases of Childhood · question page 246 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":54,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-055","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Childhood vasculitis encompasses a broad spectrum of diseases that share in common inflammation of the blood vessels. Vascular injury includes small, medium, and large vessels. Of the following, the disease that affects predominantly large blood vessels is Henoch-Schönlein purpura","o":["granulomatosis with polyangiitis (wegener granulomatosis)","childhood polyarteritis nodosa","Kawasaki disease","Takayasu arteritis 236"],"k":null,"ex":"Takayasu arteritis is the vasculitis that mainly affects large vessels (aorta and its branches). The others affect small or medium vessels.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Henoch-Schönlein purpura affects small vessels (OCR merged the stem and option A).\n\n- **B.** Granulomatosis with polyangiitis affects small to medium vessels.\n- **C.** Polyarteritis nodosa affects medium vessels.\n- **D.** Kawasaki disease affects medium vessels (coronary arteries).\n\nNelson explanation:\n55.(E).\n240\n\nHigh-yield:\nVasculitis by size: large = Takayasu; medium = PAN, Kawasaki; small = HSP, GPA.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"The OCR question text/options or source key are incomplete; the answer is disabled until checked.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Rheumatic Diseases of Childhood · question page 246 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":55,"f":true,"kx":true},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-012-3","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"An adolescent girl presented with pain beneath the patella, walking up and down stairs aggravates the pain; squatting, running, and other vigorous physical activities also exacerbate the pain. There is no history of antecedent trauma and there is no swelling. Pain is often relieved through knee extension. Of the following, the MOsT likely diagnosis is","o":["osteochondroma","osteochondritis dissecans","popliteal cyst","patellofemoral pain syndrome","juvenile idiopathic arthritis"],"k":3,"ex":"Anterior knee pain in an adolescent girl, worse on stairs, squatting and activity, with no swelling or trauma, is patellofemoral pain syndrome (chondromalacia patellae).","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Osteochondroma is a bony outgrowth, not activity-related anterior knee pain.\n\n- **B.** Osteochondritis dissecans causes pain with swelling or locking.\n- **C.** A popliteal cyst presents with posterior knee swelling.\n- **E.** JIA causes swelling and morning stiffness; this girl has no swelling.\n\nNelson explanation:\n12.(D). Patellofemoral pain syndrome (PFPS) is one of the most common causes of knee\npain, particularly in adolescent girls. The precise etiology of the knee pain remains\nunknown and is likely multifactorial.\n\nHigh-yield:\nAnterior knee pain: Osgood-Schlatter (tibial tuberosity, adolescent boys) vs patellofemoral/chondromalacia (adolescent girls, stairs).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Bone and Joint Disorders · question page 566 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":12,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-015-2","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The MosT reliable sign of a dislocated hip after the 2\"d month of life is 556 [Continuation on source page 567]","o":["limited hip abduction Bapparent shortening of the thigh asymmetry of the glutealor thigh folds Epositive Ortolani test Transient synovitis (toxic synovitis) is a reactive arthritis and is one of the most common causes of hip pain in young children. All the following clinical manifestations are true ExCEPT it is most prevalent between 3 and 8 yr of age","70% of affected children have had a preceding nonspecific upper respiratory tract infection","symptoms often develop acutely","children are usually able to bear weight on the affected limb","usually associated with a high grade fever"],"k":0,"ex":"After the second month the Ortolani/Barlow tests become unreliable; limited hip abduction is then the most reliable sign of a dislocated hip. (The block also contains a second question on transient synovitis whose key was not captured.)","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **B.** Apparent thigh shortening is less reliable and can be absent.\n\n- **C.** Asymmetric gluteal or thigh folds can occur in normal infants (OCR merged this option).\n- **E.** A positive Ortolani test is reliable only in the first weeks of life.\n\nNelson explanation:\n15.(A). As the baby enters the 2nd and 3rd mo of life, the soft tissues begin to tighten\nand the Ortolani and Barlow tests are no longer reliable. Limitation of abduction is the\nmost reliable sign of a dislocated hip in this age group.\n\nHigh-yield:\nBeyond 2–3 months: limited abduction, leg-length discrepancy (Galeazzi); use ultrasound under 4–6 months, X-ray after.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR note: this block merges two questions (hip dislocation sign, and transient synovitis \"all true EXCEPT\"). Only the first key (A) is given in the source; no source answer was captured for the transient synovitis question, so it is not answered here. Options are also merged in places.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Bone and Joint Disorders · question page 566 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":15,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-030","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The two MosT common causative organisms of osteomyelitis in children with sickle cell anemia are","o":["S. aureus and streptococci","Salmonella spp. and S. aureus","S. aureus and Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Enterobacteriaceae and S. aureus","S. aureus and Bartonella henselae"],"k":1,"ex":"In sickle cell disease the two commonest organisms in osteomyelitis are Salmonella and Staphylococcus aureus.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** S. aureus plus streptococci is the usual combination in children without sickle cell disease.\n\n- **C.** Pseudomonas is linked to foot puncture wounds.\n- **D.** Enterobacteriaceae are not the leading pair.\n- **E.** Bartonella causes cat-scratch-related disease.\n\nNelson explanation:\n30.(B). Streptococcus pneumoniae most commonly causes osteomyelitis in children\nyounger than 24 mo of age and in children with sickle cell anemia, but its frequency has\ndeclined because of pneumococcal conjugate vaccines. Cases of Pseudomonas\ninfection are related almost exclusively to puncture wounds of the foot, with direct\ninoculation of P. aeruginosa from the foam padding of the shoe into bone or cartilage,\nwhich develops as osteochondritis. Bartonella henselae can cause osteomyelitis of any\nbone, but especially in pelvic and vertebral bones.\n\nHigh-yield:\nOsteomyelitis: S. aureus overall; Salmonella in sickle cell disease; Pseudomonas after nail puncture of the foot.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Bone and Joint Disorders · question page 570 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":30,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-031","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The second MOsT common cause of osteomyelitis in children younger than 5 yr of age in some parts of the world is","o":["S. aureus","Kingella kingae","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Enterobacteriaceae","Bartonella henselae"],"k":1,"ex":"Kingella kingae is increasingly recognised as the second commonest cause of osteomyelitis in children under 5 years (detected by PCR).","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** S. aureus is the commonest, not the second.\n\n- **C.** Pseudomonas follows puncture wounds of the foot.\n- **D.** Enterobacteriaceae are mainly neonatal causes.\n- **E.** Bartonella is rare.\n\nNelson explanation:\n31.(B). Kingella kingae may be the second most common cause of osteomyelitis in\nchildren younger than 5 yr of age in some parts of the world. The organism is\nincreasingly recognized as a cause of osteomyelitis, spondylodiskitis, and septic\narthritis, especially when polymerase chain reaction testing is employed.\n\nHigh-yield:\nKingella kingae: young children, culture-negative bone/joint infection, PCR helps.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Bone and Joint Disorders · question page 570 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":31,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-032-2","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The MOsT common site of osteomyelitis in children is","o":["femur","tibia","humerus","radius","ulna"],"k":0,"ex":"The femur is the commonest site of osteomyelitis in children (23–28%), followed by the tibia.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **B.** Tibia is second (20–26%).\n\n- **C.** Humerus is less common (8–13%).\n- **D.** Radius is uncommon (5–6%).\n- **E.** Ulna is uncommon (5–6%).\n\nNelson explanation:\n32.(A). Femur 23-28%, tibia 20-26%, humerus 8-13%, radius and ulna 5-6%.\n\nHigh-yield:\nOsteomyelitis: metaphysis of long bones (distal femur, proximal tibia).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Bone and Joint Disorders · question page 570 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":32,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-038","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Osteogenesis imperfecta, the most common genetic cause of osteoporosis. Which of their types is mild and non-deforming?","o":["1","11","I1","IV","V"],"k":0,"ex":"Type I osteogenesis imperfecta is the mild, non-deforming type and the commonest one.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **B.** Type II is lethal in the perinatal period.\n\n- **C.** Type III is progressively deforming.\n- **D.** Type IV is moderately deforming.\n- **E.** Type V is moderate with hyperplastic callus.\n\nNelson explanation:\n38.(A). I: Mild, Nondeforming, II: Lethal perinatal, III: Progressively deforming, IV:\nModerately deforming\n\nHigh-yield:\nOI: blue sclera, fragile bones, hearing loss; treat with bisphosphonates.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Bone and Joint Disorders · question page 571 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":38,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-039-2","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The MosT severe non-lethal form of osteogenesis imperfecta which results in significant physical disability is type","o":["1","II","111","IV 561 [Continuation on source page 572]","V"],"k":2,"ex":"Type III is the most severe non-lethal form of osteogenesis imperfecta, with intrauterine fractures, progressive deformity and disability.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Type I is mild.\n\n- **B.** Type II is lethal, not non-lethal.\n- **D.** Type IV is moderately deforming.\n- **E.** Type V is moderate with hyperplastic callus.\n\nNelson explanation:\n39.(C). Birthweight and length are often low normal. Fractures usually occur in utero.\nThere is relative macrocephaly and triangular facies. Postnatally, fractures occur from\ninconsequential trauma and heal with deformity. Disorganization of the bone matrix\nresults in a \"popcorn\" appearance at the metaphyses. The rib cage has flaring at the\nbase, and pectal deformity is frequent. Virtually all type Ill patients have scoliosis and\nvertebral compression. Growth falls below the curve by the 1st yr; all type Ill patients\nhave extreme short stature. Scleral hue ranges from white to blue. Dentinogenesis\nimperfecta, hearing loss, and kyphoscoliosis may be present or develop over time.\n\nHigh-yield:\nOI types: I mild, II lethal, III progressively deforming (severe), IV moderate.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Bone and Joint Disorders · question page 571 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":39,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-010-3","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"It is estimated that lead-poisoned children are identified by screening procedures rather than through clinical recognition of lead related symptoms in","o":["99% of cases","79% of cases","59% of cases","39% of cases 571 [Continuation on source page 582]","19% of cases"],"k":0,"ex":"Lead poisoning is largely asymptomatic, so about 99% of affected children are found by blood lead screening rather than by symptoms.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **B.** 79% understates the role of screening.\n\n- **C.** 59% understates the role of screening.\n- **D.** 39% understates the role of screening.\n- **E.** 19% understates the role of screening.\n\nNelson explanation:\n10.(A). It is estimated that 99% of lead-poisoned children are identified by screening\nprocedures rather than through clinical recognition of lead related symptoms.\n\nHigh-yield:\nLead: screen at-risk children (blood lead level); chelation (succimer, dimercaprol/BAL, CaNa2EDTA) for high levels.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Environmental Health Hazards · question page 581 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":10,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-RHEUMATOLO-012-4","spec":"rheum","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"One of the following is not recommended for treatment of Amanita poisoning","o":["oral activated charcoal",".forced diuresis","silibinin","intravenous penicillin G","acetylcysteine"],"k":1,"ex":"Forced diuresis is not recommended in Amanita (mushroom) poisoning because it increases renal exposure. Activated charcoal, silibinin, penicillin G and N-acetylcysteine are used.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Oral activated charcoal is recommended (the option is merged with the next in the transcription).\n\n- **C.** Silibinin is used to protect the liver.\n- **D.** IV penicillin G is used.\n- **E.** N-acetylcysteine is used.\n\nNelson explanation:\n12.(B). Forced diuresis should be avoided, as this increases renal exposure.\n\nHigh-yield:\nAmanita phalloides: delayed GI symptoms then liver failure; early charcoal and supportive care.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR note: options A and B appear merged (\"oral activated charcoal . forced diuresis\"); the key (B) corresponds to forced diuresis per Nelson's explanation.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Environmental Health Hazards · question page 582 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Rheumatology / Metabolic / Poisoning / Immunology","nlNumber":12,"f":true}]);